window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-plant-growth","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Plant Growth and Development · Practice","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Plants retain the capacity for unlimited growth throughout their life. This ability is mainly due to the presence of:","image":"","options":["Meristems at certain locations in the plant body","Permanent tissues such as xylem and phloem","Mature parenchyma cells spread throughout the body","Large vacuoles in all cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the unlimited (indeterminate) growth of plants is due to meristems present at certain locations.\nMeristem cells can divide and self-perpetuate, so new cells are always being added. This is the open form of growth.\nXylem and phloem are permanent tissues whose cells have lost the ability to divide.\nMature parenchyma can divide only after dedifferentiation, which is not the basis of open growth.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is an example of dedifferentiation in plants?","image":"","options":["Formation of a tracheary element from a cambial derivative","Maturation of peripheral root cells into epidermis","Vacuolation and enlargement of cells just behind the root apex","Formation of interfascicular cambium from fully differentiated parenchyma cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Dedifferentiation means living differentiated cells that had lost the ability to divide regain that ability.\nInterfascicular cambium forms from mature parenchyma cells, so it fits this definition.\nTracheary element formation and epidermis formation are examples of differentiation.\nVacuolation and enlargement of cells behind the apex belong to the elongation phase of growth.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are effects of ethylene (or ethephon) as described in NCERT?\n(i) Horizontal growth of seedlings\n(ii) Apical hook formation in dicot seedlings\n(iii) Promotion of female flowers in cucumber\n(iv) Delay of leaf senescence through nutrient mobilisation\n(v) Sprouting of potato tubers\n(vi) Bolting in cabbage\n(vii) Root hair formation","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: ethylene causes horizontal growth of seedlings.\n(ii) Correct: it causes apical hook formation in dicot seedlings.\n(iii) Correct: ethephon promotes female flowers in cucumber.\n(iv) Incorrect: delaying senescence through nutrient mobilisation is a cytokinin effect. Ethylene actually promotes senescence.\n(v) Correct: ethylene breaks dormancy and causes potato tubers to sprout.\n(vi) Incorrect: bolting is caused by gibberellins.\n(vii) Correct: ethylene promotes root growth and root hair formation.\nSo 5 of the 7 are ethylene effects.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Dicot seedlings are grown in a sealed chamber in which ethylene accumulates to a high level. Compared with control seedlings, the treated seedlings are most likely to show:","image":"","options":["Rapid vertical elongation with thin stems and no apical hook","Bolting of internodes and early flowering","Horizontal growth, swelling of the axis and apical hook formation","Suppressed root hairs and enhanced apical dominance"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists three effects of ethylene on seedlings: horizontal growth, swelling of the axis and apical hook formation in dicot seedlings.\nOption (1) is the opposite of the expected thickened, hooked axis.\nOption (2) describes bolting, which is caused by gibberellins.\nOption (4) fails because ethylene promotes root hairs. Apical dominance is linked with auxin, and cytokinins help overcome it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"In the brewing industry, $GA_3$ is used to:","image":"","options":["Speed up the malting process","Hasten ripening of stored fruits","Prevent germination of barley grains","Kill dicotyledonous weeds in the crop"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT lists speeding up the malting process in brewing as a use of $GA_3$.\nGibberellins are growth promoters, so they do not prevent germination. Inhibiting germination is an ABA effect.\nFruit ripening is hastened by ethylene.\nKilling dicot weeds is done with the synthetic auxin 2,4-D.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Living cells growing geometrically in a natural environment give a sigmoid growth curve.\nReason (R): After the exponential phase, a limited nutrient supply slows growth down, leading to a stationary phase.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: geometric growth in a natural environment produces an S-shaped (sigmoid) curve.\nR is true: limited nutrients slow growth after the log phase, giving the stationary phase.\nR explains A: the slow lag phase, the rapid log phase and then the flattening stationary phase together make the curve S-shaped.\nThe flattening at the top comes from the stationary phase that R describes.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A plot of length against time for a root elongating at a constant rate gives a straight line.\nReason (R): In arithmetic growth, only one daughter cell continues to divide after mitosis, while the other differentiates and matures.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: constant elongation follows $L_t = L_0 + rt$, which plots as a straight line.\nR is true: in arithmetic growth, only one daughter cell keeps dividing.\nR explains A: the number of dividing cells stays constant, so the same amount of growth is added in each unit of time. This gives the linear plot.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Growth in plants is described as an open form of growth.\nReason (R): Meristematic cells retain the capacity to divide and self-perpetuate, so new cells keep being added to the plant body throughout its life.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: plants keep the capacity for unlimited growth throughout life. NCERT calls this the open (indeterminate) form of growth.\nR is true: meristem cells divide and self-perpetuate. Their products soon stop dividing and build up the plant body.\nR explains A: open growth means new cells are always being added to the plant body by meristem activity.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"In some plants, leaf shape changes because of the environment rather than the age of the plant. In which of the following does heterophylly arise from leaves developing in air versus in water?","image":"","options":["Buttercup","Cotton","Coriander","Larkspur"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Heterophylly is an example of plasticity.\nIn cotton, coriander and larkspur, juvenile leaves differ in shape from mature leaves. This is heterophylly linked to the phase of life.\nIn buttercup, leaves formed in air differ from leaves formed under water. This is heterophylly caused by the environment.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Mature seeds of many plants can survive long periods of drying. According to NCERT, abscisic acid helps seeds withstand desiccation mainly by:","image":"","options":["Promoting rapid germination before the seed dries","Inducing dormancy in the seed","Increasing the rate of respiration in the seed","Stimulating cell division in the embryo"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"ABA plays an important role in seed development, maturation and dormancy.\nBy inducing dormancy, ABA helps seeds withstand desiccation and other unfavourable factors.\nPromoting germination is the opposite of ABA's action, because ABA inhibits seed germination.\nIncreased respiration and cell division are growth-related activities, which ABA, as a growth and metabolic inhibitor, suppresses.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about plant growth rates are correct?\n(i) In $W_1 = W_0 e^{rt}$, the final size $W_1$ depends on the initial size $W_0$.\n(ii) During the exponential phase, only one daughter cell of each division continues to divide.\n(iii) A sigmoid growth curve is typical of plant cells, tissues and organs.\n(iv) Absolute growth rate is growth per unit time expressed per unit initial parameter.\n(v) In the exponential growth equation, e is the base of natural logarithms.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $W_1$ depends on $W_0$.\n(ii) Incorrect: in the log phase both progeny cells keep dividing; only one dividing describes arithmetic growth.\n(iii) Correct: the S-curve is typical of plant cells, tissues and organs.\n(iv) Incorrect: growth expressed per unit initial parameter is the relative growth rate, not the absolute one.\n(v) Correct: e is the base of natural logarithms.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Growth in plants is defined as an irreversible, permanent increase in size. Which one of the following is an example of growth?","image":"","options":["Swelling of a dry piece of wood kept in water","Increase in size of guard cells during stomatal opening","A wilted leaf regaining turgidity after watering","Expansion of a young leaf"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Growth is an irreversible, permanent increase in size. It is usually driven by anabolic and catabolic metabolism that uses energy.\nExpansion of a leaf is permanent and metabolically driven, so it is growth.\nA swelling piece of wood is dead tissue taking up water physically. This change is reversible.\nThe swelling of guard cells and the recovery of a wilted leaf are temporary turgor changes, so they are not growth either.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Spraying a sugarcane crop with gibberellins can raise yield by as much as 20 tonnes per acre. This happens because gibberellins:","image":"","options":["Increase the number of tillers by breaking apical dominance","Increase the length of the stem, which stores sugar","Convert stored starch in roots into sucrose","Delay flowering so that the leaves photosynthesise longer"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Sugarcane stores its carbohydrate as sugar in the stem.\nGibberellins increase the length of the axis, so the stem becomes longer.\nA longer stem stores more sugar, which raises yield by up to 20 tonnes per acre.\nOvercoming apical dominance is a cytokinin effect. The other two options are not the reason NCERT gives.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are effects of auxins as described in NCERT?\n(i) Initiation of roots in stem cuttings\n(ii) Bolting in cabbage\n(iii) Control of xylem differentiation\n(iv) Abscission of older, mature leaves and fruits\n(v) Speeding up the malting process in brewing\n(vi) Prevention of fruit and leaf drop at early stages\n(vii) Promotion of female flowers in cucumber","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Auxin effect: auxins initiate rooting in stem cuttings.\n(ii) Not auxin: bolting is caused by gibberellins.\n(iii) Auxin effect: auxins control xylem differentiation.\n(iv) Auxin effect: auxins promote abscission of mature organs.\n(v) Not auxin: GA3 speeds up malting.\n(vi) Auxin effect: auxins prevent early fruit and leaf drop.\n(vii) Not auxin: ethephon (an ethylene source) promotes female flowers in cucumber.\nTotal = 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following in a woody dicotyledonous stem are products of redifferentiation?\n(i) Secondary xylem\n(ii) Secondary phloem\n(iii) Cork (phellem)\n(iv) Secondary cortex (phelloderm)\n(v) Interfascicular cambium\n(vi) Callus formed from parenchyma in tissue culture","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Redifferentiated cells are produced by dedifferentiated meristems; they lose the capacity to divide and mature to perform specific functions.\n(i), (ii) Yes: they are produced by the vascular cambium, which includes the dedifferentiated interfascicular cambium.\n(iii), (iv) Yes: they are produced by cork cambium, which forms by dedifferentiation.\n(v) No: it is itself a product of dedifferentiation.\n(vi) No: callus is a mass of undifferentiated, dividing cells (dedifferentiated).\nTotal = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"The 'bakanae' or foolish seedling disease of rice, which led to the discovery of gibberellins, is caused by:","image":"","options":["Gibberella fujikuroi, a fungus","Agrobacterium tumefaciens, a bacterium","Tobacco mosaic virus","Puccinia, a rust fungus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Bakanae disease of rice seedlings is caused by the fungal pathogen Gibberella fujikuroi.\nE. Kurosawa (1926) reproduced the disease symptoms by treating rice seedlings with sterile filtrates of this fungus.\nThe active substances in the filtrate were later identified as gibberellic acid.\nThe other options are pathogens not linked to this discovery.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Two similar pea plants are decapitated. On the cut stem tip of Plant X, a paste containing IAA is applied. On the cut tip of Plant Y, plain paste without IAA is applied. Which outcome is most likely, given that auxin is produced at the shoot apex and is responsible for apical dominance?","image":"","options":["Lateral buds grow in both X and Y","Lateral buds grow in X but remain suppressed in Y","Lateral buds remain suppressed in X but grow out in Y","Lateral buds remain suppressed in both X and Y"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Auxin is produced at the growing apex and moves down the stem. There it inhibits the axillary buds; this is apical dominance.\nPlant Y has lost its source of auxin. Its inhibition is released, so its lateral buds grow out, just as after normal decapitation.\nIn Plant X, the IAA paste replaces the auxin that the apex used to supply. The lateral buds therefore stay suppressed.\nThis comparison shows that auxin from the apex causes apical dominance.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"The term auxin comes from the Greek word 'auxein', meaning to grow. From which source was auxin first isolated?","image":"","options":["Coconut milk","Autoclaved herring sperm DNA","Human urine","Sterile filtrate of Gibberella fujikuroi"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that auxins were first isolated from human urine.\nCoconut milk is a source of zeatin, a natural cytokinin.\nAutoclaved herring sperm DNA gave kinetin, the first cytokinin discovered.\nThe Gibberella fujikuroi filtrate led to the discovery of gibberellic acid.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is an example of dedifferentiation in plants?","image":"","options":["Root-cap cells forming from cells of the root apical meristem","Cork cambium arising from fully differentiated parenchyma cells","Secondary xylem forming from the vascular cambium","Tracheids maturing from procambial cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Dedifferentiation means that living differentiated cells, which had lost the ability to divide, regain it.\nNCERT's examples are interfascicular cambium and cork cambium, which form from fully differentiated parenchyma cells.\nOptions (1) and (4) are differentiation of cells that come from meristems.\nOption (3) is the production of cells by an existing meristem, which then mature.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"In the exponential growth equation $W_1 = W_0 e^{rt}$, the term $r$ is also known as the:","image":"","options":["Absolute growth rate","Efficiency index","Lag constant","Elongation per unit time"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In $W_1 = W_0 e^{rt}$, $r$ is the relative growth rate.\nIt measures the plant's ability to produce new plant material, so it is called the efficiency index.\nThe absolute growth rate is total growth per unit time, which is a different quantity.\n'Elongation per unit time' is the meaning of $r$ in the arithmetic equation $L_t = L_0 + rt$, not here.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are naturally occurring auxins that have been isolated from plants?\nIAA, IBA, NAA, 2,4-D, zeatin, kinetin","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"IAA: natural auxin isolated from plants. Counts.\nIBA: natural auxin isolated from plants. Counts.\nNAA and 2,4-D: these are auxins, but they are synthetic.\nZeatin: a natural cytokinin, not an auxin.\nKinetin: a cytokinin that does not occur naturally in plants.\nSo only 2 items fit.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Callus from internodal segments of tobacco stem proliferated only when the auxin-containing medium was also supplemented with coconut milk, yeast extract, vascular tissue extract or DNA.\nReason (R): Kinetin, the cytokinesis-promoting substance crystallised by Miller et al., occurs naturally and abundantly in plant tissues such as coconut milk.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Skoog and co-workers observed that tobacco callus proliferated only when one of these supplements was added along with auxin.\nR is false. Kinetin was crystallised from autoclaved herring sperm DNA and does not occur naturally in plants.\nThe natural cytokinin found in coconut milk and corn kernels is zeatin.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which pair of meristems causes the increase in girth (secondary growth) of organs in dicotyledonous plants and gymnosperms?","image":"","options":["Root apical meristem and shoot apical meristem","Shoot apical meristem and vascular cambium","Vascular cambium and cork cambium","Root apical meristem and cork cambium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: In dicots and gymnosperms, the lateral meristems appear later in life. These are the vascular cambium and the cork cambium, and they increase the girth of the organs in which they are active. This is secondary growth.\nThe root and shoot apical meristems cause primary growth, which means elongation along the axis.\nOptions (1), (2) and (4) all include an apical meristem, so they are wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (effect of ethylene or ethephon) with List-II (plant in which NCERT cites it):\nList-I\nA. Initiates seed germination\nB. Induces flowering\nC. Thinning by accelerating fruit abscission\nD. Rapid internode/petiole elongation\nList-II\nI. Mango\nII. Deep water rice\nIII. Peanut\nIV. Cherry","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-III: ethylene initiates germination in peanut seeds.\nB-I: ethylene induces flowering in mango.\nC-IV: ethephon accelerates abscission, which is used for thinning of cotton, cherry and walnut.\nD-II: ethylene causes rapid internode/petiole elongation in deep water rice, keeping the upper parts above water.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of cells in the meristematic phase of growth?","image":"","options":["Rich in protoplasm with large conspicuous nuclei","Thin, cellulosic primary cell walls","Abundant plasmodesmatal connections","Increased vacuolation and new cell wall deposition"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Meristematic cells are rich in protoplasm and have large conspicuous nuclei, so (1) is a feature.\nThey have thin, primary, cellulosic walls, so (2) is a feature.\nThey have abundant plasmodesmata, so (3) is a feature.\nIncreased vacuolation, cell enlargement and new wall deposition mark the elongation phase, so (4) is not a meristematic feature.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Plants retain the capacity for unlimited growth throughout their life.\nReason (R): All cells of a plant body retain the capacity to divide throughout the life of the plant.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Plant growth is generally indeterminate, which is the open form of growth.\nR is false. Only meristem cells keep dividing and self-perpetuating.\nThe cells produced by meristems soon lose the capacity to divide, and these cells make up the plant body.\nUnlimited growth therefore comes from meristems, not from division of every cell.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Canary grass coleoptiles were given unilateral light under four conditions:\n(a) intact coleoptile\n(b) tip cut off\n(c) tip covered with an opaque cap\n(d) tip exposed but the region below covered with an opaque sleeve\nThe coleoptiles in (a) and (d) bent towards the light, while those in (b) and (c) did not. The most valid conclusion is:","image":"","options":["Light is perceived by the region below the tip, which itself bends","The tip is the site of a transmittable influence that causes bending of the entire coleoptile","Bending occurs only at the tip because the region below lacks growth","The bending is caused by ethylene released from the illuminated side of the base"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Removing the tip (b) or shading it (c) abolished bending, so light is perceived at the tip.\nShading the lower region (d) did not stop bending, so the lower region is not the light sensor.\nThe bending takes place below the tip, so an influence must travel from the tip to the region that bends.\nThis matches the Darwins' conclusion: the tip is the site of a transmittable influence.\nOptions (1) and (3) contradict the results, and ethylene plays no part in this experiment.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (auxin-related effect or use) with List-II (example):\nList-I\n(a) Parthenocarpy\n(b) Promotion of flowering\n(c) Practical use of decapitation\n(d) Preparation of weed-free lawns\nList-II\n(i) Tea plantations\n(ii) 2,4-D\n(iii) Tomato\n(iv) Pineapple\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Auxins induce parthenocarpy in tomato, so (a) matches (iii).\n(b) Auxins promote flowering in pineapple, so (b) matches (iv).\n(c) Decapitation removes apical dominance and is used in tea plantations, so (c) matches (i).\n(d) 2,4-D kills dicot weeds but spares mature monocots (grasses), giving weed-free lawns, so (d) matches (ii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Many extrinsic factors such as light and temperature control events like vernalisation, flowering and seed germination by acting through plant growth regulators.\nStatement II: Events such as abscission, senescence and apical dominance are each controlled by a single plant growth regulator acting individually.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT says many extrinsic factors, such as temperature and light, control growth via PGRs. Examples include vernalisation, flowering, dormancy, seed germination and plant movements.\nStatement II is incorrect: NCERT names dormancy, abscission, senescence and apical dominance as events where more than one PGR interacts. These interactions can be complementary, antagonistic or synergistic.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are effects of ethylene on plants?\n(i) Horizontal growth of seedlings\n(ii) Closure of stomata\n(iii) Bolting in cabbage\n(iv) Sprouting of potato tubers\n(v) Delay of leaf senescence\n(vi) Root hair formation","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Ethylene: it causes horizontal growth of seedlings.\n(ii) Not ethylene: ABA stimulates stomatal closure.\n(iii) Not ethylene: gibberellins promote bolting in rosette plants.\n(iv) Ethylene: it initiates sprouting of potato tubers.\n(v) Not ethylene: cytokinins delay senescence, whereas ethylene promotes it.\n(vi) Ethylene: it promotes root growth and root hair formation.\nSo three effects belong to ethylene.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"In plant tissue culture, parenchyma cells are made to divide under controlled conditions to form a callus. A callus is best described as:","image":"","options":["A mass of undifferentiated cells formed by dedifferentiation","A fully redifferentiated organ","A group of dead tracheary elements","A cluster of root-cap cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT defines callus as a mass of undifferentiated cells, for example the callus from tobacco internodal segments in Skoog's work.\nThe parenchyma cells were differentiated and could not divide. Regaining the ability to divide is dedifferentiation.\nThe callus has not yet redifferentiated into organs, so option (2) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Auxins help prevent the dropping of fruits and leaves at early stages of their development.\nStatement II: Auxins promote the abscission of older, mature leaves and fruits.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT states that auxins help prevent fruit and leaf drop at early stages.\nStatement II is also correct: the same auxins promote abscission of older, mature leaves and fruits.\nThe two statements do not contradict each other. Auxin's effect on abscission depends on the developmental stage of the organ.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Lag phase\n(b) Exponential phase\n(c) Stationary phase\n(d) Arithmetic growth\nList-II:\n(i) Growth slows down due to limited nutrient supply\n(ii) Initial slow growth\n(iii) Constant rate of growth giving a linear curve\n(iv) Both progeny cells keep dividing, giving rapid growth\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Lag phase: initial growth is slow, so (ii).\n(b) Exponential phase: both progeny cells divide and growth is rapid, so (iv).\n(c) Stationary phase: limited nutrients slow growth down, so (i).\n(d) Arithmetic growth: constant rate and a linear curve, so (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A cell produced by the shoot apical meristem matures into a cortical parenchyma cell. Years later, in the woody stem, it regains the ability to divide and forms cork cambium. The cork cambium then cuts off cells that mature into cork. Which sequence correctly names these three events in order?","image":"","options":["Dedifferentiation → Differentiation → Redifferentiation","Differentiation → Redifferentiation → Dedifferentiation","Differentiation → Dedifferentiation → Redifferentiation","Redifferentiation → Dedifferentiation → Differentiation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: The meristem-derived cell matures into parenchyma. This is differentiation.\nStep 2: The differentiated parenchyma regains division capacity and becomes cork cambium. This is dedifferentiation.\nStep 3: Cells cut off by cork cambium lose the ability to divide and mature as cork. This is redifferentiation.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Heterophylly in buttercup results from the phase of life of the plant, whereas heterophylly in larkspur results from the environment","Kinetin was obtained from autoclaved herring sperm DNA and does not occur naturally in plants","Formation of interfascicular cambium from fully differentiated parenchyma cells is an example of dedifferentiation","Auxins prevent fruit and leaf drop at early stages but promote abscission of older mature leaves and fruits"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: the causes are reversed. Larkspur, cotton and coriander show heterophylly linked to the phase of life (juvenile versus mature leaves). Buttercup shows heterophylly due to environment (leaves in air versus leaves in water).\n(2) Correct: kinetin is a modified adenine from herring sperm DNA and is not natural to plants. Zeatin is a natural cytokinin.\n(3) Correct: differentiated parenchyma regains the ability to divide, which is dedifferentiation.\n(4) Correct: NCERT states this dual role of auxins.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"The 'bakanae' or foolish seedling disease of rice, which led to the discovery of gibberellins, is caused by:","image":"","options":["Gibberella fujikuroi","Fusarium oxysporum","Puccinia graminis","Ustilago maydis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Infected rice seedlings grow abnormally tall and weak, which is why the disease is called 'foolish seedling'.\nNCERT states that bakanae disease is caused by the fungal pathogen Gibberella fujikuroi.\nThe active substance from this fungus was later identified as gibberellic acid.\nThe other three are fungal pathogens, but they are not linked to gibberellin discovery.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Differentiation in plants is described as open.\nReason (R): Cells arising from the same meristem can have different structures at maturity, and their final structure is also determined by their location.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that differentiation in plants, like growth, is open.\nR is true. Cells from one meristem mature differently depending on position, for example as root-cap cells or as epidermis.\nR is exactly why differentiation is called open, so it correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about ethylene is incorrect?","image":"","options":["It breaks seed and bud dormancy and causes sprouting of potato tubers","It is used to initiate flowering and synchronise fruit-set in pineapples","It delays the abscission of leaves and flowers","It promotes root growth and root hair formation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Option (1) is correct: ethylene breaks seed and bud dormancy and causes sprouting of potato tubers.\nOption (2) is correct: ethylene initiates flowering and synchronises fruit-set in pineapple.\nOption (3) is incorrect: ethylene promotes senescence and abscission of plant organs, especially leaves and flowers; it does not delay them.\nOption (4) is correct: ethylene promotes root growth and root hairs, increasing the absorption surface.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Apple growers spray gibberellins so that the fruits can be left on the tree for a longer time, extending the market period.\nReason (R): Gibberellins delay senescence.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: NCERT states that gibberellins delay senescence.\nA is true: because senescence is delayed, the fruits can stay on the tree longer and the market period is extended.\nSo the delay of senescence (R) is exactly why fruits can be left on the tree (A). R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Open form of growth\n(b) Primary growth\n(c) Secondary growth\n(d) Growth\nList-II\n(i) Increase in girth due to vascular cambium and cork cambium\n(ii) Irreversible permanent increase in size of an organ, its part or a cell\n(iii) New cells continuously added to the plant body by meristem activity\n(iv) Elongation of the plant along its axis by root and shoot apical meristems","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(iii): In the open form of growth, meristems keep adding new cells to the plant body.\n(b)-(iv): Primary growth is elongation along the axis by root and shoot apical meristems.\n(c)-(i): Secondary growth is the increase in girth caused by the lateral meristems, vascular cambium and cork cambium.\n(d)-(ii): Growth is an irreversible, permanent increase in size.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"In the NCERT diagram of the developmental sequence of a plant cell, a meristematic cell undergoes cell division. Which stage comes immediately after cell division and before expansion (elongation)?","image":"","options":["Plasmatic growth","Maturation","Senescence","Differentiation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The order is: cell division → plasmatic growth → expansion (elongation) → maturation/differentiation → mature cell → senescence → death.\nPlasmatic growth is the increase in protoplasm. It lies between division and expansion.\nMaturation and differentiation come after expansion.\nSenescence occurs only in the mature cell.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Differentiation in plants is described as open.\nReason (R): Cells produced by the root apical meristem mature as root-cap cells or as epidermis depending on their position.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Cells or tissues that arise from the same meristem have different structures at maturity, so differentiation is called open.\nR is true. Cells positioned away from the root apical meristem become root-cap cells, while cells pushed to the periphery mature as epidermis.\nR shows that the final fate of a cell depends on its location. This is exactly why differentiation is open, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Root apical and shoot apical meristems are responsible for primary growth.\n(b) Primary growth principally contributes to elongation of the plant along its axis.\n(c) Vascular cambium is an apical meristem located at the shoot tip.\n(d) Secondary growth increases the girth of the organs in which lateral meristems are active.\n(e) In dicots and gymnosperms, lateral meristems are present right from the embryo stage and are active from the beginning of growth.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: the root and shoot apical meristems bring about primary growth.\n(b) Correct: primary growth mainly elongates the plant along its axis.\n(c) Incorrect: vascular cambium is a lateral meristem, not an apical one.\n(d) Correct: lateral meristems increase girth, which is secondary growth.\n(e) Incorrect: NCERT says lateral meristems appear later in life.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about gibberellins are correct?\n(i) More than 100 gibberellins have been reported from organisms such as fungi and higher plants.\n(ii) All gibberellins are basic in nature.\n(iii) $GA_3$ was one of the first gibberellins discovered and is the most intensively studied.\n(iv) Chemically, gibberellic acid is a terpene.\n(v) Gibberellin spray reduces stem length in sugarcane.","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Three","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: over 100 GAs are known from fungi and higher plants.\n(ii) Incorrect: all GAs are acidic, not basic.\n(iii) Correct: $GA_3$ was among the first discovered and is the most studied form.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT groups $GA_3$ under terpenes.\n(v) Incorrect: GA increases stem length in sugarcane.\nThat makes three correct statements.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"ABA acts antagonistic to","image":"","options":["Ethylene","Cytokinin","Gibberellic acid","IAA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: ABA is called the stress hormone. It induces seed and bud dormancy and inhibits seed germination.\nGibberellins do the opposite. They break dormancy and promote germination, for example by inducing $\\alpha$-amylase in cereal grains.\nFor this reason NCERT states that ABA acts as an antagonist to GAs.\nWhy not ethylene: ethylene is not a GA-type antagonist of ABA. Ethylene and ABA both promote senescence and abscission. ABA does also oppose cytokinin and auxin in some effects, but the NCERT pairing is with GA.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 15 (MCQ Q10)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Auxins can induce the development of fruits without fertilisation (parthenocarpy). NCERT gives this example in:","image":"","options":["Tomato","Cucumber","Sugarcane","Beet"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states that auxins induce parthenocarpy, for example in tomatoes.\nIn cucumber, ethephon (a source of ethylene) promotes female flowers.\nIn sugarcane, gibberellins increase stem length and therefore yield.\nIn beet, gibberellins promote bolting.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (application) with List-II (purpose achieved by gibberellins):\nList-I\n(a) Spraying juvenile conifers\n(b) Treating beet and cabbage\n(c) Spraying apple fruits\n(d) Using GA$_3$ in the brewing industry\nList-II\n(i) Fruit elongation and improved shape\n(ii) Faster malting process\n(iii) Hastened maturity and early seed production\n(iv) Bolting, i.e., internode elongation just before flowering","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(iii): Spraying GAs on juvenile conifers hastens their maturity, so they produce seeds early.\n(b)-(iv): Gibberellins promote bolting in rosette plants such as beet and cabbage.\n(c)-(i): GAs make apple fruits elongate and improve their shape.\n(d)-(ii): GA$_3$ speeds up malting in the brewing industry.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (plant growth regulator) with List-II (chemical nature).\nList-I:\n(a) Indole-3-acetic acid\n(b) Kinetin\n(c) Abscisic acid\n(d) Gibberellic acid ($GA_3$)\nList-II:\n(i) Terpene\n(ii) Derivative of carotenoids\n(iii) Indole compound\n(iv) Adenine derivative\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) IAA is an indole compound, so it matches (iii).\n(b) Kinetin is N6-furfurylamino purine, an adenine derivative, so it matches (iv).\n(c) ABA is derived from carotenoids, so it matches (ii).\n(d) $GA_3$ is a terpene, so it matches (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A horticulturist has the following goals:\n(i) Hasten ripening of tomatoes and apples\n(ii) Thin out the fruits on walnut trees\n(iii) Increase the length of grape stalks\n(iv) Increase the number of female flowers in cucumber\n(v) Delay senescence so that apples stay on the tree longer\nFor which of these goals would ethephon be the appropriate choice?","image":"","options":["(i), (iii) and (v) only","(i), (ii) and (iv) only","(ii), (iii) and (iv) only","(i), (ii), (iv) and (v) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: ethephon hastens fruit ripening in tomatoes and apples.\n(ii) Yes: ethephon accelerates abscission of fruits, which is used for thinning in walnut, cherry and cotton.\n(iii) No: increasing the length of grape stalks is a gibberellin effect.\n(iv) Yes: ethephon promotes female flowers in cucumber.\n(v) No: delaying senescence to extend the market period of fruits is done with gibberellins. Ethylene promotes senescence.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a root, the zone undergoing maturation lies farther away from the apex than the zone of elongation.\nStatement II: Cells of the maturation zone attain their maximal size in terms of wall thickening and protoplasmic modifications.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The order from the tip is meristematic, then elongation, then maturation. The maturation zone is more proximal, i.e., farther from the apex.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that maturation-zone cells attain maximal size in terms of wall thickening and protoplasmic modifications.\nMost familiar tissues and cell types represent this phase.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Natural cytokinins are synthesised mainly in:","image":"","options":["Root apices, developing shoot buds and young fruits","Senescing leaves and ripening fruits","Mature xylem vessels","Dormant seeds under water stress"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Natural cytokinins are made in regions where rapid cell division occurs. Examples are root apices, developing shoot buds and young fruits.\nSenescing tissues and ripening fruits are major sites of ethylene synthesis.\nMature xylem vessels are dead cells, so they cannot synthesise hormones.\nDormancy under stress is associated with ABA.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Seedlings are kept in a well-watered, nutrient-rich medium at their optimum temperature, but in an atmosphere completely lacking oxygen. Their growth is severely hampered. Based on NCERT, the best explanation is that:","image":"","options":["Cells cannot maintain turgidity, because oxygen is the medium for enzymatic activity","Release of the metabolic energy essential for growth activities is impaired","The temperature falls below the optimum range in the absence of oxygen","Nutrients can no longer act as a source of energy because water is unavailable"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Water, nutrients and temperature are all favourable here, so only oxygen is lacking.\nNCERT states that oxygen helps release the metabolic energy essential for growth activities.\nGrowth is an energy-consuming metabolic process, so a lack of energy hampers it.\nOption (1) is wrong because water, not oxygen, maintains turgidity and provides the medium for enzymes.\nOption (3) is wrong because oxygen does not set the temperature.\nOption (4) is wrong because water is available.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"In 1910, H.H. Cousins confirmed that a volatile substance released from ripened oranges hastened the ripening of stored unripe bananas. This substance was later identified as:","image":"","options":["Abscisic acid","Gibberellic acid","Ethylene","Indole-3-acetic acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Cousins observed that a volatile substance from ripened oranges hastened the ripening of stored bananas.\nA volatile substance is a gas, and the only gaseous PGR is ethylene ($C_2H_4$).\nEthylene is also highly effective in fruit ripening, which fits the observation.\nThe other options are not volatile.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A student lists four observations in plants. Which one should NOT be considered growth according to the NCERT definition?","image":"","options":["A maize root apical meristem producing thousands of new cells every hour","A dorsiventral leaf showing a steady increase in surface area as it expands","A flaccid parenchyma cell swelling in water and then shrinking back to its original volume when moved to a concentrated salt solution","A pollen tube increasing in length as it grows through the style"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Growth is an irreversible, permanent increase in size, generally accompanied by metabolic processes that use energy.\n(1) An increase in cell number is growth, as in the maize root meristem.\n(2) Leaf expansion, measured as an increase in area, is growth.\n(4) Pollen tube growth is measured as an increase in length.\n(3) The swelling is purely osmotic and is fully reversed in the salt solution. It is not a permanent increase, so it is not growth, even though turgidity does support real extension growth.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Three identical potted plants are treated as follows:\nPlant P: shoot tip left intact.\nPlant Q: shoot tip cut off and the cut surface capped with plain agar.\nPlant R: shoot tip cut off and the cut surface capped with agar containing IAA.\nAfter some days, in which plant(s) would the axillary buds most likely grow into branches?","image":"","options":["Only in Q","In both Q and R","Only in P","Only in R"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The growing apex produces auxin, which moves down the stem and inhibits the lateral buds. This is apical dominance.\nP: the apex is intact, so the lateral buds stay suppressed.\nQ: decapitation removes the auxin source and plain agar supplies none, so the lateral buds grow.\nR: the IAA in the agar replaces the auxin from the lost tip, so dominance is maintained.\nThe tempting option (2) ignores that applied IAA substitutes for the apex.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A root elongates at a constant rate. If its length is plotted against time, which curve is obtained?","image":"","options":["A sigmoid (S-shaped) curve","A J-shaped exponential curve","A straight line (linear curve)","A bell-shaped curve"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A root elongating at a constant rate is the simplest example of arithmetic growth.\nIt follows $L_t = L_0 + rt$, which is the equation of a straight line.\nSo plotting length against time gives a linear curve.\nA sigmoid curve is the tempting wrong choice, but it belongs to geometric growth with lag, log and stationary phases.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Nutrients (macro- and micro-essential elements) are required by plants for the synthesis of protoplasm and also act as a source of energy.\nStatement II: Environmental signals such as light and gravity have no effect on plant growth, because growth is controlled only by intrinsic factors.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT says nutrients are needed to synthesise protoplasm and also act as a source of energy.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT states that light and gravity affect certain phases or stages of growth.\nGrowth is controlled by both intrinsic and extrinsic factors, not by intrinsic factors alone.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the phases of growth in a root tip are correct?\n(i) Cells in the meristematic phase have thin, primary, cellulosic walls with abundant plasmodesmatal connections.\n(ii) Increased vacuolation, cell enlargement and new cell wall deposition are features of the maturation phase.\n(iii) The zone of elongation lies just proximal to (away from the tip than) the meristematic zone.\n(iv) In the parallel line technique, the marks farthest from the root apex show the greatest separation after growth.\n(v) Cells of the maturation zone attain their maximal size in terms of wall thickening and protoplasmic modifications.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. Meristematic cells are rich in protoplasm, have large nuclei and thin primary cellulosic walls with many plasmodesmata.\n(ii) Incorrect. Vacuolation, enlargement and new wall deposition characterise the elongation phase, not maturation.\n(iii) Correct. The elongation zone lies immediately proximal to the meristematic zone.\n(iv) Incorrect. Zones just behind the apex elongate most, not the distant ones.\n(v) Correct. Maturation-zone cells reach maximal wall thickening and protoplasmic modification.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about cytokinins is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Cytokinins have specific effects on cytokinesis.","Kinetin occurs naturally and abundantly in plant tissues.","Cytokinins help in the formation of chloroplasts in leaves.","Cytokinins promote adventitious shoot formation."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: cytokinins were named for their specific effect on cytokinesis.\n(2) Incorrect: NCERT states that kinetin does not occur naturally in plants. This is why scientists searched for natural cytokinins such as zeatin.\n(3) Correct: cytokinins help produce chloroplasts in leaves.\n(4) Correct: cytokinins promote adventitious shoot formation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are synthetic auxins?\nIAA, IBA, NAA, 2,4-D, zeatin, gibberellic acid","image":"","options":["2","3","4","1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"IAA and IBA are natural auxins that have been isolated from plants, so they do not count.\nNAA (naphthalene acetic acid) and 2,4-D are synthetic auxins, so they count.\nZeatin is a natural cytokinin and gibberellic acid is a gibberellin, so neither is an auxin.\nThe total is 2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about geometric growth are correct?\n(i) A sigmoid curve is characteristic of a living organism growing in a natural environment.\n(ii) The stationary phase results from a limited nutrient supply.\n(iii) In the exponential phase, only one daughter cell of each division keeps dividing.\n(iv) For cells in culture, the growth curve is a straight line from the very beginning.\n(v) According to $W_1 = W_0 e^{rt}$, the final size depends on the initial size.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT calls the S-curve characteristic of organisms in a natural environment.\n(ii) Correct: limited nutrients slow growth, producing the stationary phase.\n(iii) Incorrect: in the exponential phase both progeny cells divide; only one dividing is arithmetic growth.\n(iv) Incorrect: cells in culture show an idealised sigmoid curve, not a straight line.\n(v) Correct: $W_1$ depends on $W_0$.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (plant growth regulator) with List-II (chemical nature).\nList-I:\n(A) Indole-3-acetic acid\n(B) Kinetin\n(C) Abscisic acid\n(D) Gibberellic acid ($GA_3$)\nList-II:\n(i) Derivative of carotenoids\n(ii) Indole compound\n(iii) Terpene\n(iv) Adenine derivative","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) IAA is an indole compound, so A-ii.\n(B) Kinetin ($N^6$-furfurylamino purine) is an adenine derivative, so B-iv.\n(C) ABA is a derivative of carotenoids, so C-i.\n(D) $GA_3$ is a terpene, so D-iii.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A mutant plant cannot synthesise abscisic acid but is otherwise normal. Based on the roles of ABA described in NCERT, which set of observations is most likely?","image":"","options":["Its seeds remain dormant even under favourable conditions, and its stomata stay permanently closed","Its seeds show poor dormancy and poor desiccation tolerance, and its leaves wilt readily in drought because stomata fail to close","It shows greater tolerance to drought and salinity than normal plants","Its seeds germinate slowly because gibberellin action is enhanced"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"ABA induces seed dormancy and, through dormancy, gives desiccation tolerance. Without ABA, seeds lack dormancy and cope poorly with drying.\nABA stimulates stomatal closure. Without it, stomata stay open during drought, water is lost and the leaves wilt.\nOption 1 describes excess ABA, not its absence.\nOption 3 is wrong because ABA raises stress tolerance, so the mutant should be less tolerant.\nOption 4 fails because unopposed GA would favour faster germination, not slower.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"F. Skoog and co-workers cultured internodal segments of tobacco stems on a nutrient medium. Based on their observations, which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The callus formed was a mass of undifferentiated cells","Callus proliferated when auxin was supplemented with coconut milk","Callus proliferated when coconut milk alone was added, without any auxin","The active substance responsible was later identified as kinetin, which promotes cytokinesis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT defines callus as a mass of undifferentiated cells.\n(2) Correct: auxin plus coconut milk (or vascular tissue extract, yeast extract or DNA) allowed callus to proliferate.\n(3) Incorrect: proliferation occurred only if these supplements were added in addition to auxins. Coconut milk without auxin does not fit the observation.\n(4) Correct: Miller et al. (1955) identified the active substance as kinetin.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"In a young dicot tree, the shoot apical meristem of the main stem is destroyed by an insect. The vascular cambium and cork cambium of the stem remain active. What is most likely to happen to the main stem afterwards?","image":"","options":["It will stop both elongating and thickening","It will continue to elongate but stop thickening","It will stop elongating at its tip but continue to increase in girth","It will continue both elongation and thickening at the same rate as before"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The shoot apical meristem is responsible for primary growth, that is, elongation along the axis. Once it is destroyed, the main axis stops elongating at its tip.\nThe vascular cambium and cork cambium are lateral meristems that cause secondary growth, an increase in girth. They are still active, so the stem keeps thickening.\nOption (2) reverses the roles of the two meristem types.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following is NOT a role of water in plant growth, as described in NCERT?","image":"","options":["Maintaining turgidity of cells, which helps in extension growth","Providing the medium for enzymatic activities needed for growth","Being required for cell enlargement","Releasing metabolic energy essential for growth activities"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Plant cells grow by cell enlargement, and enlargement requires water.\nTurgidity of cells helps in extension growth.\nWater also provides the medium in which the enzymes needed for growth act.\nReleasing metabolic energy is the role of oxygen, not water. So option (4) is the one that does not fit.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Leaf P grows from 5 $cm^2$ to 10 $cm^2$, while leaf Q grows from 50 $cm^2$ to 55 $cm^2$ in the same period. What is the ratio of the relative growth rate of P to that of Q, taking relative growth rate as increase in area per unit initial area per unit time?","image":"","options":["1","5","10","0.1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: P gains 5 $cm^2$ on 5 $cm^2$; Q gains 5 $cm^2$ on 50 $cm^2$, in the same time.\nFormula: relative growth rate = increase ÷ initial size (per unit time).\nSubstituting: P = 5/5 = 1; Q = 5/50 = 0.1.\nRatio = 1/0.1 = 10.\nA ratio of 1 is wrong because that compares absolute growth rates, which are equal here.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"The effect of apical dominance can be overcome by which of the following hormone:","image":"","options":["IAA","Ethylene","Gibberellin","Cytokinin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: in apical dominance, auxin (IAA) made in the shoot apex suppresses growth of the lateral (axillary) buds.\nCytokinins act against this effect. They promote growth of lateral shoots and so help overcome apical dominance. So (4) fits.\nIAA (1) is the tempting option, but IAA is the hormone that causes apical dominance, not the one that removes it.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 15 (MCQ Q3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Plasticity in plant growth means that","image":"","options":["Plant roots are extensible","Plant development is dependent on the environment","Stems can extend","None of the above"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): plasticity is the ability of plants to follow different pathways in response to the environment or phases of life, forming different kinds of structures.\nExample: heterophylly in cotton, coriander and larkspur (leaf shape changes with age), and in buttercup (leaf shape changes with habitat).\nSo plant development depends on the environment, and (2) fits.\nOptions (1) and (3) only describe elongation, not developmental flexibility.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 15 (MCQ Q8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (plant growth regulator) with List-II (source of first isolation/identification):\nList-I\n(a) Kinetin\n(b) Zeatin\n(c) Auxin\n(d) Gibberellic acid\nList-II\n(i) Human urine\n(ii) Gibberella fujikuroi\n(iii) Autoclaved herring sperm DNA\n(iv) Corn kernels","image":"","options":["a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii","a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Kinetin was obtained from autoclaved herring sperm DNA, so a-iii.\n(b) Zeatin was isolated from corn kernels and coconut milk, so b-iv.\n(c) Auxin was first isolated from human urine, so c-i.\n(d) Gibberellic acid came from the fungus Gibberella fujikuroi, so d-ii.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which sequence correctly represents the developmental process of a plant cell, as shown in NCERT?","image":"","options":["Cell division → Expansion → Plasmatic growth → Maturation → Death → Senescence","Cell division → Plasmatic growth → Expansion → Maturation → Senescence → Death","Plasmatic growth → Cell division → Maturation → Expansion → Senescence → Death","Cell division → Maturation → Plasmatic growth → Expansion → Senescence → Death"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The meristematic cell first divides.\nThe daughter cell then shows plasmatic growth, followed by expansion (elongation).\nNext comes maturation (differentiation), which produces the mature cell.\nThe mature cell undergoes senescence and finally death.\nOption (1) wrongly places expansion before plasmatic growth and death before senescence.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are effects of cytokinins?\n(i) Production of new leaves\n(ii) Formation of chloroplasts in leaves\n(iii) Lateral shoot growth\n(iv) Adventitious shoot formation\n(v) Strengthening of apical dominance\n(vi) Bolting in rosette plants\n(vii) Induction of seed dormancy","image":"","options":["Three","Five","Four","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) to (iv) Correct: all four are effects of cytokinins listed in NCERT.\n(v) Wrong: cytokinins help overcome apical dominance, not strengthen it.\n(vi) Wrong: bolting is caused by gibberellins.\n(vii) Wrong: inducing seed dormancy is a role of ABA.\nSo the total is four.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Kinetin is a cytokinin that promotes cytokinesis.\nReason (R): Kinetin was first isolated from corn kernels and coconut milk, where it occurs naturally.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Kinetin, a modified adenine (purine), has a specific cytokinesis-promoting effect.\nR is false. Kinetin was obtained from autoclaved herring sperm DNA and does not occur naturally in plants.\nThe substance isolated from corn kernels and coconut milk was zeatin, a natural cytokinin.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (growing system) with List-II (how its growth is expressed/measured) as described in NCERT.\nList-I\n(a) Maize root apical meristem\n(b) Watermelon cells\n(c) Pollen tube\n(d) Dorsiventral leaf\nList-II\n(i) Increase in surface area\n(ii) Increase in cell number\n(iii) Increase in length\n(iv) Increase in cell size","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) A maize root apex produces more than 17,500 new cells per hour, so its growth is increase in cell number: (ii).\n(b) Watermelon cells enlarge up to 3,50,000 times, so their growth is increase in cell size: (iv).\n(c) Pollen tube growth is measured in terms of its length: (iii).\n(d) Growth of a dorsiventral leaf is denoted by increase in surface area: (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Plant part)\n(A) Pollen tube\n(B) Dorsiventral leaf\n(C) Maize root apical meristem\n(D) Watermelon cell\nList-II (Growth expressed as)\n(I) Increase in cell number\n(II) Increase in surface area\n(III) Increase in cell size\n(IV) Increase in length\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Pollen tube: its growth is measured as length, so A-IV.\n(B) Dorsiventral leaf: its growth is denoted by an increase in surface area, so B-II.\n(C) Maize root apex: it produces more than 17,500 new cells per hour, so growth is expressed as cell number, C-I.\n(D) Watermelon cell: it enlarges up to 3,50,000 times, so growth is expressed as cell size, D-III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Growth in plants is generally measured using parameters such as fresh weight, dry weight, length, area, volume and cell number.\nReason (R): Growth at the cellular level is mainly an increase in protoplasm, which is difficult to measure directly.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT lists fresh weight, dry weight, length, area, volume and cell number as parameters for measuring growth.\nR is true. Cellular growth is principally an increase in the amount of protoplasm, and this is hard to measure directly.\nBecause protoplasm cannot easily be measured, we measure quantities roughly proportional to it. So R explains why A is done.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Leaf A has an initial area of 5 $cm^2$ and leaf B has an initial area of 50 $cm^2$. In the same time interval, each leaf increases its area by 5 $cm^2$.\nStatement I: The two leaves have equal absolute growth rates, but the relative growth rate of leaf A is ten times that of leaf B.\nStatement II: In the exponential growth equation $W_1 = W_0 e^{rt}$, r is the absolute growth rate, and hence the final size $W_1$ does not depend on the initial size $W_0$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Absolute growth rate is the total growth per unit time. Both leaves gain 5 $cm^2$, so their absolute growth rates are equal.\nRelative growth rate is the growth per unit initial area. For A it is $\\frac{5}{5} = 1$, and for B it is $\\frac{5}{50} = 0.1$. So A's relative rate is 10 times B's, and Statement I is correct.\nIn $W_1 = W_0 e^{rt}$, r is the relative growth rate (the efficiency index), not the absolute rate. The final size $W_1$ depends on $W_0$, so Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"In which plant does heterophylly arise because of the environment, with leaves produced in air differing in shape from leaves produced in water?","image":"","options":["Cotton","Coriander","Larkspur","Buttercup"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Buttercup produces leaves of different shapes in air and under water. This is heterophylly caused by the environment.\nIn cotton, coriander and larkspur, the leaves of the juvenile plant differ from those of the mature plant. That heterophylly is linked to the phase of life, not the environment.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is an intercellular intrinsic factor that controls plant development?","image":"","options":["Plant growth regulators","Light","Temperature","Oxygen"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Intrinsic factors are of two kinds:\n- intracellular factors, which are genetic;\n- intercellular factors, which are chemicals such as plant growth regulators (PGRs).\nLight, temperature and oxygen are extrinsic (external) factors.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Abscisic acid is also called the stress hormone.\nReason (R): Abscisic acid stimulates closure of stomata and increases the tolerance of plants to various kinds of stresses.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT refers to ABA as the stress hormone.\nR is true: ABA closes stomata and raises tolerance to different stresses.\nNCERT gives the name stress hormone precisely because of these stress-protective actions, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"In an experiment similar to that of the Darwins, canary grass coleoptiles are lit from one side only. The results are:\n(a) intact coleoptile bends towards the light;\n(b) coleoptile with tip removed does not bend;\n(c) coleoptile with tip covered by an opaque cap does not bend;\n(d) coleoptile with only the lower part covered and the tip exposed bends towards the light.\nWhich conclusion is best supported by these results?","image":"","options":["The lower region of the coleoptile perceives light and bends independently of the tip","Light directly stimulates elongation of cells on the illuminated side of the coleoptile","The tip perceives the light and is the source of a transmittable influence that causes bending of the region below","The Darwins isolated auxin from the coleoptile tip, which shows that auxin causes bending"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Result (b) shows that bending needs the tip.\nResult (c) shows that the tip must receive light.\nResult (d) shows that shading the lower part does not prevent bending. So the light is sensed at the tip, but the bending happens below it. Some influence must therefore travel from the tip.\nBending towards light actually means the shaded side grows more, which rules out option (2).\nThe Darwins isolated nothing; auxin was isolated later by Went.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding gibberellins is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The symptoms of 'bakanae' disease of rice were produced by sterile filtrates of Gibberella fujikuroi, whose active substance was gibberellic acid","In most situations, abscisic acid acts as an antagonist to gibberellins","Gibberellins are modified adenine compounds first obtained from autoclaved herring sperm DNA","Gibberellins promote bolting in plants with a rosette habit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: Kurosawa showed that fungal filtrates caused bakanae symptoms, and the active substance was later identified as gibberellic acid.\n(2) Correct: NCERT notes that ABA mostly acts as an antagonist to GAs.\n(3) Incorrect: the adenine derivative from autoclaved herring sperm DNA is kinetin, a cytokinin. Gibberellins are acidic terpenes.\n(4) Correct: GA causes bolting in rosette plants.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are plant growth promoters, according to the NCERT grouping of PGRs?\nIAA, $GA_3$, abscisic acid, kinetin, indole butyric acid, dormin, zeatin","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT groups auxins, gibberellins and cytokinins as growth promoters.\nIAA and IBA are auxins, so they are promoters.\n$GA_3$ is a gibberellin, so it is a promoter.\nKinetin and zeatin are cytokinins, so they are promoters.\nAbscisic acid is a growth inhibitor.\nDormin was later shown to be chemically identical to ABA, so it is also an inhibitor.\nPromoters = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A horticulturist wants the axillary buds of a potted shrub to grow into branches. However, she does not want to remove the growing shoot tip. Which treatment of the axillary buds is most likely to work?","image":"","options":["Applying IAA paste to the axillary buds","Applying a cytokinin solution to the axillary buds","Spraying abscisic acid on the axillary buds","Applying NAA to the shoot apex"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Apical dominance means auxin from the growing apex keeps the lateral buds inhibited.\nCytokinins help overcome apical dominance and promote lateral shoot growth. Applying them to the buds can release the buds even with the tip intact.\nIAA or NAA would reinforce the inhibition, as auxin is the cause of apical dominance.\nABA is a general growth inhibitor.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Spraying a sugarcane crop with gibberellins can raise its yield by as much as 20 tonnes per acre. Which effect of gibberellins is responsible for this?","image":"","options":["Increase in the length of the stem, which stores carbohydrate as sugar","Delay of leaf senescence through nutrient mobilisation","Induction of parthenocarpic fruits","Stimulation of root hair formation for better absorption"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: sugarcane stores carbohydrate as sugar in its stem.\nGibberellins increase the length of the axis, so a sprayed crop develops longer stems. More stem means more stored sugar, which raises the yield by up to 20 tonnes per acre.\nOption (2) describes cytokinins, and (3) is an auxin effect seen in tomatoes.\nOption (4) is an effect of ethylene.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"The formation of interfascicular cambium from fully differentiated parenchyma cells in a dicot stem is an example of:","image":"","options":["Differentiation","Dedifferentiation","Redifferentiation","Senescence"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Parenchyma cells are living, differentiated cells that have lost the ability to divide.\nWhen they regain the ability to divide and become a meristem (interfascicular cambium), the process is called dedifferentiation.\nRedifferentiation is the tempting wrong option. It refers to the later step, when cells produced by this new meristem mature again into specialised cells.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A single maize root apical meristem can produce more than 17,500 new cells per hour","Growth of a watermelon fruit is largely expressed as an increase in the size of its cells","Constantly dividing cells at both the root apex and the shoot apex represent the meristematic phase","Cells of the maturation zone are best identified by increased vacuolation and new cell wall deposition"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT gives a figure of more than 17,500 new cells per hour for one maize root apical meristem.\n(2) Correct: watermelon cells may enlarge up to 3,50,000 times, so growth there is expressed as cell size.\n(3) Correct: constantly dividing apical cells represent the meristematic phase.\n(4) Incorrect: increased vacuolation and new wall deposition characterise the elongation phase. Maturation cells show maximal wall thickening and protoplasmic modifications.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about cytokinins.\nStatement I: Kinetin is a naturally occurring cytokinin that was first isolated from corn kernels.\nStatement II: Natural cytokinins are synthesised in regions of rapid cell division, such as root apices, developing shoot buds and young fruits.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Kinetin was discovered in autoclaved herring sperm DNA, and it does not occur naturally in plants.\nZeatin is the cytokinin that was isolated from corn kernels and coconut milk.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that natural cytokinins are made where rapid cell division occurs, for example root apices, developing shoot buds and young fruits.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following effects are attributed to ethylene (or its source, ethephon) in NCERT?\n(a) Horizontal growth of seedlings\n(b) Apical hook formation in dicot seedlings\n(c) Inhibition of seed germination\n(d) Promotion of female flowers in cucumber\n(e) Bolting in cabbage\n(f) Promotion of root hair formation\n(g) Delay of leaf senescence","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(a) Yes: ethylene causes horizontal growth of seedlings.\n(b) Yes: it causes apical hook formation in dicot seedlings.\n(c) No: ethylene breaks seed dormancy. It is ABA that inhibits seed germination.\n(d) Yes: ethephon promotes female flowers in cucumber.\n(e) No: bolting is caused by gibberellins.\n(f) Yes: ethylene promotes root growth and root hair formation.\n(g) No: ethylene promotes senescence, whereas cytokinins delay it.\nThe total is 4.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Growth is generally accompanied by both anabolic and catabolic processes that occur at the expense of energy.\n(ii) Vascular cambium and cork cambium are lateral meristems.\n(iii) Root and shoot apical meristems principally contribute to an increase in girth.\n(iv) Cells produced by meristems soon lose the capacity to divide and make up the plant body.\n(v) In dicots, lateral meristems are present from the very beginning of seedling growth.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: growth involves anabolic and catabolic metabolism that uses energy.\n(ii) Correct: vascular cambium and cork cambium are lateral meristems.\n(iii) Incorrect: apical meristems mainly cause elongation along the axis, not an increase in girth.\n(iv) Correct: the products of meristems soon stop dividing and form the plant body.\n(v) Incorrect: NCERT says lateral meristems appear later in life.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Application of cytokinins to detached leaves delays their yellowing.\nReason (R): Cytokinins promote nutrient mobilisation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: cytokinins delay leaf senescence, so a treated leaf stays green longer.\nR is true: cytokinins promote nutrient mobilisation.\nNCERT directly links the two. Nutrient mobilisation is the reason leaf senescence is delayed, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Leaf A grows from 5 cm$^2$ to 10 cm$^2$ and leaf B grows from 50 cm$^2$ to 55 cm$^2$ in the same time. Consider the statements:\n(i) Both leaves show the same absolute growth rate.\n(ii) Leaf A shows a higher relative growth rate than leaf B.\n(iii) Leaf B shows a higher relative growth rate because its final area is larger.\n(iv) Relative growth rate expresses growth per unit time on a common basis, such as per unit initial parameter.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["0","1","2","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: each leaf gains 5 cm$^2$ in the same time.\n(ii) Correct: A's relative growth rate is 5/5 = 100%, while B's is 5/50 = 10%.\n(iii) Incorrect: a larger final size does not mean a larger relative rate. B's rate is lower.\n(iv) Correct: this is the NCERT definition of relative growth rate.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Plant growth is commonly measured using parameters such as fresh weight, dry weight, length, area, volume and cell number.\nReason (R): Increase in protoplasm, the real basis of growth, is difficult to measure directly, so quantities roughly proportional to it are measured.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT lists fresh weight, dry weight, length, area, volume and cell number as growth parameters.\nR is true: growth is principally an increase in protoplasm, which is hard to measure directly.\nR explains A: these parameters are used because they are more or less proportional to the increase in protoplasm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"In the exponential growth equation $W_1 = W_0 e^{rt}$, the term $r$ measures the ability of the plant to produce new plant material. What is $r$ also called?","image":"","options":["Absolute growth rate","Efficiency index","Elongation constant","Lag coefficient"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In $W_1 = W_0 e^{rt}$, $r$ is the relative growth rate.\nIt measures the plant's ability to produce new plant material, and NCERT calls it the efficiency index.\nAbsolute growth rate is wrong because it means total growth per unit time, not growth expressed per unit initial size.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Tracheary elements must be living cells with active protoplasm to conduct water.\nStatement II: The lignocellulosic secondary walls of tracheary elements allow them to carry water over long distances even under extreme tension.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Tracheary elements lose their protoplasm during differentiation, so they are dead at maturity.\nStatement II is correct. Their strong, elastic, lignocellulosic secondary walls let them conduct water over long distances under extreme tension.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Since kinetin was obtained from DNA, a molecule present in every plant cell, kinetin is a natural cytokinin of plants.\nStatement II: Zeatin, isolated from corn kernels and coconut milk, is a naturally occurring cytokinin.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Kinetin is a modified adenine obtained from autoclaved herring sperm DNA. NCERT clearly states that kinetin does not occur naturally in plants, so its DNA origin does not make it a plant cytokinin.\nStatement II is correct. The search for natural cytokinin-like substances led to zeatin from corn kernels and coconut milk.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Dedifferentiated cells permanently retain the capacity to divide and never mature again","During differentiation, cells may undergo changes in both cell wall and protoplasm","A tumour can be viewed as cells that have regained division but go on dividing without maturing into functional tissue","Cork cambium can arise from differentiated parenchyma cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect. Dedifferentiated meristems produce cells that again lose the capacity to divide and mature into specialised cells. This is redifferentiation.\n(2) Correct. Differentiation involves few to major changes in both wall and protoplasm.\n(3) Correct. A tumour shows uncontrolled division of dedifferentiated cells without proper redifferentiation.\n(4) Correct. NCERT cites cork cambium arising from parenchyma.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A seedling root is 5 cm long at the start of an observation. It grows arithmetically with an elongation rate of 0.8 cm per day. What will its length be after 6 days?","image":"","options":["4.8 cm","9.8 cm","24.0 cm","5.8 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $L_0$ = 5 cm, $r$ = 0.8 cm/day, $t$ = 6 days.\nFormula: $L_t = L_0 + rt$\nSubstituting: $L_t = 5 + (0.8 \\times 6) = 5 + 4.8$\nSo $L_t$ = 9.8 cm.\nThe option 4.8 cm is only the growth added in 6 days; it leaves out the initial length.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, F.W. Went isolated auxin from which of the following?","image":"","options":["Coleoptiles of canary grass","Tips of coleoptiles of oat seedlings","Internodal segments of tobacco stems","Sterile filtrates of a fungus infecting rice"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The Darwins worked with canary grass coleoptiles and showed only that the tip transmits an influence. They did not isolate any substance.\nF.W. Went later isolated auxin from the tips of oat seedling coleoptiles.\nTobacco internodes were used by Skoog in callus work. Fungal filtrates on rice relate to the discovery of gibberellins.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"The term synergistic action of hormones refers to","image":"","options":["When two hormones act together but bring about opposite effects.","When two hormones act together and contribute to the same function.","When one hormone affects more than one function.","When many hormones bring about any one function."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: synergism means two hormones act together and reinforce the same physiological response. An example is auxin and gibberellin both promoting stem elongation.\nOption (2) describes exactly this.\nOption (1) is the tempting wrong choice. Hormones that produce opposite effects are antagonistic, not synergistic.\nOption (3) describes a single hormone with several roles (pleiotropy), so it is not synergism either.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 15 (MCQ Q7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Lag phase\n(b) Exponential (log) phase\n(c) Stationary phase\n(d) Arithmetic growth\nList-II\n(i) Growth slows because of limited nutrient supply\n(ii) Initial slow growth\n(iii) Constant elongation rate giving a linear plot\n(iv) Both progeny cells retain the ability to divide","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii","a-ii, b-i, c-iv, d-iii","a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Lag phase is the initial slow growth, so it matches (ii).\n(b) In the log phase, both progeny cells keep dividing, so it matches (iv).\n(c) The stationary phase occurs when growth slows due to limited nutrients, so it matches (i).\n(d) Arithmetic growth has a constant rate and a linear plot, so it matches (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Buttercup shows heterophylly, which is an example of plasticity.\nReason (R): In buttercup, the leaves of the juvenile plant differ in shape from those of the mature plant.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: buttercup shows heterophylly, and NCERT cites heterophylly as an example of plasticity.\nR is false: buttercup leaves differ between those produced in air and those produced in water. This difference is due to the environment.\nThe juvenile versus mature difference applies to cotton, coriander and larkspur.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Cotton\nB. Buttercup\nC. Plant growth regulators\nD. Light\nList-II\n(i) Extrinsic factor of development\n(ii) Intercellular intrinsic factor\n(iii) Heterophylly: juvenile vs mature leaves\n(iv) Heterophylly: aerial vs submerged leaves","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Cotton shows different leaf shapes in juvenile and mature plants.\nB-(iv): Buttercup leaves formed in air differ from those formed in water.\nC-(ii): PGRs are intercellular intrinsic (chemical) factors.\nD-(i): Light is an extrinsic factor.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Cells in a watermelon may increase in size by up to:","image":"","options":["17,500 times","3,500 times","35,000 times","3,50,000 times"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that cells in a watermelon may increase in size by up to 3,50,000 times.\nHere growth is expressed as an increase in cell size.\nThe figure 17,500 is a different fact: it is the number of new cells produced per hour by a maize root apical meristem.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs consists only of synthetic auxins?","image":"","options":["IAA and IBA","NAA and 2,4-D","IAA and NAA","IBA and 2,4-D"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"IAA (indole-3-acetic acid) and IBA (indole butyric acid) are natural auxins. Both have been isolated from plants.\nNAA (naphthalene acetic acid) and 2,4-D (2,4-dichlorophenoxyacetic acid) are synthetic auxins.\nOptions (3) and (4) each pair one natural auxin with one synthetic auxin, so they are wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a given time, a leaf that grows from 5 $cm^2$ to 10 $cm^2$ shows a higher relative growth rate than a leaf that grows from 50 $cm^2$ to 55 $cm^2$.\nReason (R): Relative growth rate is the growth per unit time expressed per unit initial parameter.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Both leaves gain 5 $cm^2$, so their absolute growth rates are equal.\nThe small leaf gains 5 $cm^2$ on an initial 5 $cm^2$, which is a 100% increase.\nThe large leaf gains 5 $cm^2$ on an initial 50 $cm^2$, which is only a 10% increase. So A is true.\nR is NCERT's definition of relative growth rate. It is exactly why the small leaf's rate is higher, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which compound is most widely used in agriculture as a source of ethylene?","image":"","options":["Naphthalene acetic acid (NAA)","2, 4-D","Ethephon","Zeatin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ethephon is readily absorbed from an aqueous solution and transported within the plant. It then releases ethylene slowly.\nThis makes it the most widely used source of ethylene.\nNAA and 2, 4-D are synthetic auxins.\nZeatin is a natural cytokinin.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Ethylene is used for","image":"","options":["Retarding ripening of tomatoes","Hastening of ripening of fruits","Slowing down ripening of apples","Both b and c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): ethylene is a gaseous hormone that promotes fruit ripening. It increases respiration rate during ripening (the respiratory climacteric).\nCommercially, ethylene (or ethephon, which releases it) is used to hasten ripening of fruits such as tomatoes and apples. So (2) fits.\nOptions (1) and (3) describe the opposite effect, slowing ripening, which ethylene does not cause. Option (4) includes the false option (3), so it is also wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 15 (MCQ Q1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Growth can be measured in various ways. Which of these can be used as parameters to measure growth","image":"","options":["Increase in cell number","Increase in cell size","Increase in length and weight","All the above"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: growth is an irreversible permanent increase in size, mass or cell number.\nNCERT lists several ways to measure it. These include increase in cell number (e.g. maize root apex), increase in cell size (e.g. watermelon cells), length (e.g. pollen tube), and fresh or dry weight, area and volume.\nAll three listed parameters are valid, so (4) fits.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 15 (MCQ Q6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the phases of growth at a root tip is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Cells of the meristematic phase are rich in protoplasm and have large, conspicuous nuclei","Increased vacuolation, cell enlargement and new cell wall deposition characterise the phase of elongation","Cells of the meristematic phase have thick secondary walls with very few plasmodesmatal connections","Cells of the maturation phase attain their maximal size in terms of wall thickening and protoplasmic modifications"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: meristematic cells are rich in protoplasm and have large nuclei.\n(2) Correct: these are the stated features of the elongation phase.\n(3) Incorrect: meristematic cells have thin, primary, cellulosic walls with abundant plasmodesmatal connections, not thick secondary walls.\n(4) Correct: maturation-zone cells reach their maximal size in wall thickening and protoplasmic modifications.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A potted plant is left without water for several days. Which plant growth regulator is mainly responsible for closing its stomata to reduce water loss?","image":"","options":["Gibberellic acid","Abscisic acid","Cytokinin","Indole-3-acetic acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Water shortage is a stress, and the plant must cut down transpiration.\nNCERT states that abscisic acid (ABA) stimulates the closure of stomata.\nGibberellins, cytokinins and auxins are growth promoters. None of them is described as closing stomata.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: The leaves of a juvenile larkspur plant differ in shape from those of the mature plant. This is heterophylly arising from different phases of life, and it is an example of plasticity.\nStatement II: In buttercup, the different leaf shapes are controlled only by intrinsic genetic factors, so leaves formed in water and in air have the same shape.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Plasticity is the ability of plants to follow different developmental pathways in response to the environment or to phases of life, forming different structures.\nStatement I is correct. In larkspur, cotton and coriander, juvenile leaves differ in shape from mature leaves. This is heterophylly linked to the phase of life.\nStatement II is incorrect. In buttercup, leaves produced in water differ in shape from those produced in air. This heterophylly is caused by the environment, not by genetic factors alone.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are effects of ethylene on plants?\n(i) Horizontal growth of seedlings\n(ii) Apical hook formation in dicot seedlings\n(iii) Swelling of the axis\n(iv) Bolting in cabbage\n(v) Delay of leaf senescence\n(vi) Root hair formation","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: ethylene causes horizontal growth of seedlings.\n(ii) Correct: it causes apical hook formation in dicot seedlings.\n(iii) Correct: it causes swelling of the axis.\n(iv) Incorrect: bolting is promoted by gibberellins.\n(v) Incorrect: ethylene promotes senescence; cytokinins delay it.\n(vi) Correct: ethylene promotes root growth and root hair formation.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the mid-1960s, inhibitor-B, abscission II and dormin were purified by three independent researches and were later proved to be chemically identical.\nStatement II: This single compound was named abscisic acid and is placed among the plant growth promoters.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Three independent groups characterised inhibitor-B, abscission II and dormin, and all three were later found to be the same chemical.\nStatement II is incorrect. The compound was indeed named abscisic acid (ABA). However, ABA belongs to the group of growth inhibitors involved in dormancy, abscission and stress responses, not to the promoters.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are extrinsic factors controlling plant development?\nLight, temperature, plant growth regulators, oxygen, genetic factors, water, nutrition","image":"","options":["4","6","5","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Extrinsic factors: light, temperature, oxygen, water and nutrition. That makes 5.\nIntrinsic factors: genetic factors (intracellular) and plant growth regulators (intercellular).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Interfascicular cambium and cork cambium arise from fully differentiated parenchyma cells that regain the capacity to divide.\nStatement II: Cells produced by such meristems lose the capacity to divide again and mature to perform specific functions, which is called redifferentiation.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Living differentiated cells regaining division capacity is dedifferentiation. NCERT gives interfascicular cambium and cork cambium as examples.\nStatement II is correct. The products of these meristems lose division capacity again and mature for specific functions, which is redifferentiation.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (scientist) with List-II (contribution).\nList-I:\n(a) Charles and Francis Darwin\n(b) E. Kurosawa\n(c) F. Skoog and Miller\n(d) H.H. Cousins\nList-II:\n(i) Volatile substance from ripened oranges hastening banana ripening\n(ii) Cytokinesis-promoting substance needed for callus proliferation from tobacco internodes\n(iii) Bakanae symptoms in rice seedlings produced by sterile fungal filtrates\n(iv) Coleoptiles of canary grass bending towards unilateral light","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i","a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i","a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a)-(iv): The Darwins studied phototropism in canary grass coleoptiles. This work eventually led to the discovery of auxin.\n(b)-(iii): Kurosawa showed that sterile filtrates of Gibberella fujikuroi caused bakanae symptoms. The active substance was gibberellic acid.\n(c)-(ii): Skoog's callus work and Miller's crystallisation identified kinetin.\n(d)-(i): Cousins observed that ripened oranges released a volatile substance, later identified as ethylene.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"In arithmetic growth, what happens to the two daughter cells formed after a mitotic cell division?","image":"","options":["Both daughter cells keep dividing","Only one daughter cell keeps dividing while the other differentiates and matures","Both daughter cells stop dividing and differentiate","Both daughter cells enlarge without dividing again"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: in arithmetic growth, only one daughter cell keeps dividing after mitosis. The other differentiates and matures.\nThe number of dividing cells therefore stays the same, so growth occurs at a constant rate.\nOption (1) is tempting, but it describes geometric growth, where both progeny cells keep dividing.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (plant response) with List-II (plant growth regulator):\nList-I\n(a) Stomatal closure under water stress\n(b) Breaking of seed dormancy and sprouting of potato tubers\n(c) Delay of leaf senescence\n(d) Bolting in cabbage\nList-II\n(i) Gibberellin\n(ii) Cytokinin\n(iii) Abscisic acid\n(iv) Ethylene","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Stomatal closure under stress is caused by ABA, so (iii).\n(b) Ethylene breaks seed and bud dormancy and causes potato tubers to sprout, so (iv).\n(c) Cytokinins promote nutrient mobilisation and delay leaf senescence, so (ii).\n(d) Gibberellins promote bolting in beet and cabbage, so (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A root is 2 cm long at time zero and elongates at a constant rate of 0.5 cm per day. What will its length be after 6 days?","image":"","options":["3 cm","5 cm","8 cm","12 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $L_0$ = 2 cm, $r$ = 0.5 cm/day, $t$ = 6 days.\nFormula: constant elongation is arithmetic growth, so $L_t = L_0 + rt$.\nSubstituting: $L_t = 2 + 0.5 \\times 6 = 2 + 3$.\nSo $L_t$ = 5 cm.\nNote that 3 cm is only the elongation, not the final length.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, a single root apical meristem of maize can give rise to more than how many new cells per hour?","image":"","options":["1,750","17,500","35,000","3,50,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT gives one maize root apical meristem as producing more than 17,500 new cells per hour.\nHere growth is expressed as an increase in cell number.\nThe figure 3,50,000 is a distractor. It belongs to a different example: a watermelon cell can enlarge up to 3,50,000 times. That is growth by increase in cell size, not cell number.\nThe other two values do not appear in the text.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which option gives the correct sequence of phases in a typical sigmoid growth curve?","image":"","options":["Exponential phase → Lag phase → Stationary phase","Lag phase → Stationary phase → Exponential phase","Stationary phase → Lag phase → Exponential phase","Lag phase → Exponential phase → Stationary phase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Lag phase: initial growth is slow.\nExponential (log) phase: growth then rises rapidly because both progeny cells keep dividing.\nStationary phase: with limited nutrient supply, growth slows down.\nPlotting a growth parameter against time therefore gives an S-curve in the order lag → log → stationary.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In tea plantations, the shoot tips are regularly plucked to obtain a bushy plant with many branches.\nReason (R): The growing apical bud inhibits the growth of lateral (axillary) buds, so removing it allows the lateral buds to grow.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that decapitation is widely applied in tea plantations and in hedge-making.\nR is true. The growing apical bud suppresses the axillary buds, a phenomenon called apical dominance.\nOnce the shoot tip is removed, the lateral buds are released and grow into branches, giving a bushy plant. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (scientist) with List-II (observation that led to discovery of a PGR).\nList-I:\nA. Charles and Francis Darwin\nB. E. Kurosawa\nC. F. Skoog and co-workers\nD. H.H. Cousins\nList-II:\n(i) A volatile substance from ripened oranges hastened ripening of stored bananas\n(ii) Callus from tobacco internodal segments proliferated only with auxin plus supplements such as coconut milk\n(iii) Symptoms of 'bakanae' disease appeared in rice treated with sterile fungal filtrates\n(iv) Coleoptiles of canary grass bent towards unilateral light","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A-(iv): The Darwins studied phototropism in canary grass coleoptiles, which led to auxin.\nB-(iii): Kurosawa (1926) studied bakanae of rice, which led to gibberellic acid.\nC-(ii): Skoog worked with tobacco callus; Miller et al. later identified kinetin.\nD-(i): Cousins (1910) observed the volatile from oranges, later identified as ethylene.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"The ability of plants to follow different pathways in response to the environment or to phases of life, forming different kinds of structures, is called:","image":"","options":["Dedifferentiation","Redifferentiation","Plasticity","Apical dominance"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT defines plasticity as the ability of plants to form different structures in response to the environment or phases of life. Heterophylly is an example.\nDedifferentiation is the regaining of division capacity by differentiated cells.\nRedifferentiation is the renewed loss of division capacity by cells produced from such tissues.\nApical dominance is the inhibition of lateral buds by the apical bud.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Inhibitor-B, abscission II and dormin, reported by three independent research groups in the mid-1960s, are today referred to by a single name.\nReason (R): These three inhibitors were later proved to be chemically identical and were named abscisic acid.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the three inhibitors purified independently are now known collectively by one name.\nR is true: NCERT states that all three were proved chemically identical and named abscisic acid (ABA).\nR directly explains why a single name is used for all three.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"In most situations, abscisic acid acts as an antagonist to which group of plant growth regulators?","image":"","options":["Gibberellins","Ethylene","Synthetic auxins such as 2,4-D","Inhibitor-B and dormin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT states directly that, in most situations, ABA acts as an antagonist to GAs.\nFor example, GAs promote growth and ABA inhibits it.\nEthylene is largely a growth inhibitor, much like ABA, so it is not its typical antagonist.\nInhibitor-B and dormin were later shown to be chemically identical to ABA itself.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following substances belong to the plant growth promoter group of PGRs?\nIAA, $GA_3$, ABA, kinetin, zeatin, dormin","image":"","options":["3","4","5","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"IAA is an auxin, so it is a promoter.\n$GA_3$ is a gibberellin, so it is a promoter.\nABA is a growth inhibitor.\nKinetin is a cytokinin, so it is a promoter.\nZeatin is a natural cytokinin, so it is a promoter.\nDormin was shown to be identical to ABA, so it is an inhibitor.\nTotal promoters = 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Scientist) with List-II (Contribution):\nList-I\n(A) Charles and Francis Darwin\n(B) E. Kurosawa\n(C) Miller et al.\n(D) H.H. Cousins\nList-II\n(i) Volatile substance from ripened oranges\n(ii) Crystallised kinetin\n(iii) Phototropism in canary grass coleoptiles\n(iv) Sterile fungal filtrate producing disease symptoms in rice","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: The Darwins observed canary grass coleoptiles bending towards unilateral light, so A-(iii).\nB: Kurosawa (1926) produced bakanae symptoms in rice using sterile filtrates of the fungus, so B-(iv).\nC: Miller et al. (1955) identified and crystallised kinetin, so C-(ii).\nD: Cousins (1910) worked with the volatile substance from ripened oranges, so D-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A root elongates at a constant rate. If its length is plotted against time, what type of curve is obtained?","image":"","options":["Sigmoid (S-shaped) curve","Bell-shaped curve","Linear curve","Exponential (J-shaped) curve"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A root elongating at a constant rate is NCERT's simplest example of arithmetic growth.\nIt follows $L_t = L_0 + rt$, which is the equation of a straight line.\nSo a plot of length against time gives a linear curve.\nThe sigmoid curve is wrong because it belongs to geometric growth, which has lag, log and stationary phases.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"At the cellular level, growth is principally a consequence of an increase in the amount of:","image":"","options":["cell wall material","protoplasm","vacuolar sap","intercellular spaces"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that growth at the cellular level is principally a consequence of an increase in the amount of protoplasm.\nWall material and vacuolar sap do increase in some phases, such as elongation. They are not the basic measure of cellular growth.\nProtoplasm is difficult to measure directly, so a quantity roughly proportional to it is measured instead.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Leaf A grows from 5 cm$^2$ to 10 cm$^2$. In the same time, leaf B grows from 50 cm$^2$ to 55 cm$^2$. Which statement correctly compares their growth?","image":"","options":["Both leaves have the same relative growth rate, but B has a higher absolute growth rate","B has both higher absolute and higher relative growth rate because it is larger","Both leaves have identical absolute and relative growth rates","Both leaves have the same absolute growth rate, but A has ten times the relative growth rate of B"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Absolute growth rate is the total increase per unit time. Both leaves gain 5 cm$^2$, so their absolute growth rates are equal.\nRelative growth rate is the increase per unit initial size.\nFor A: 5/5 = 1, which is 100%.\nFor B: 5/50 = 0.1, which is 10%.\nSo A's relative growth rate is ten times that of B.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Plant growth regulators belong to chemically diverse groups. Which of the following PGRs is a derivative of carotenoids?","image":"","options":["Indole-3-acetic acid","Kinetin","Abscisic acid","Gibberellic acid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT groups PGRs by chemical nature. IAA is an indole compound, kinetin is an adenine derivative, ABA is derived from carotenoids, GA3 is a terpene and ethylene is a gas.\nGibberellic acid is the tempting wrong choice because both carotenoids and terpenes are isoprenoid-related. NCERT, however, classes GA3 as a terpene, not a carotenoid derivative.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Ethylene is one of the most widely used plant growth regulators in agriculture.\nReason (R): Ethylene regulates a large number of physiological processes such as ripening, abscission, dormancy and flowering.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT describes ethylene as one of the most widely used PGRs in agriculture.\nR is true: ethylene influences ripening, senescence, abscission, dormancy breaking, flowering, root hair formation and more.\nNCERT states directly that ethylene is widely used because it regulates so many processes. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"In the root tip, cells derived from the root apical meristem that are pushed towards the periphery mature as:","image":"","options":["Root-cap cells","Epidermis","Xylem vessels","Pericycle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In plants, the final structure of a cell depends on where it is located.\nCells pushed to the periphery mature as epidermis.\nCells positioned away from the root apical meristem, towards the tip, become root-cap cells.\nRoot-cap cells are the tempting wrong option because they also come from the root apical meristem, but they form at a different position.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are effects of ethylene described in NCERT?\n(i) Apical hook formation in dicot seedlings\n(ii) Rapid internode/petiole elongation in deep water rice\n(iii) Breaking of dormancy and sprouting of potato tubers\n(iv) Stimulation of stomatal closure under water stress\n(v) Promotion of root growth and root hair formation\n(vi) Bolting in cabbage and beet","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: ethylene causes apical hook formation in dicot seedlings.\n(ii) Correct: ethylene elongates internodes and petioles in deep water rice, which keeps the upper parts of the shoot above water.\n(iii) Correct: ethylene breaks bud dormancy and causes potato tubers to sprout.\n(iv) Incorrect: stomatal closure is stimulated by ABA, the stress hormone.\n(v) Correct: ethylene promotes root growth and root hair formation, which increases the absorption surface.\n(vi) Incorrect: bolting in rosette plants is caused by gibberellins.\nSo 4 of the statements are ethylene effects.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Two leaves, A (small) and B (large), each increase in area by 5 $cm^2$ in the same period of time.\nStatement I: Leaves A and B have the same absolute growth rate.\nStatement II: Leaf A has a higher relative growth rate than leaf B.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: absolute growth rate is total growth per unit time. Both leaves gained 5 $cm^2$ in the same time, so this rate is equal.\nStatement II is correct: relative growth rate is growth per unit initial size. The same 5 $cm^2$ forms a larger fraction of the smaller leaf A.\nThe two statements together reflect NCERT's leaves A and B comparison.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"In a woody dicotyledonous plant, how many of the following are products of redifferentiation?\nSecondary xylem, secondary phloem, cork (phellem), phelloderm (secondary cortex), primary phloem, root-cap cells","image":"","options":["2","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Secondary xylem and secondary phloem are produced by vascular cambium. Its interfascicular part arises from parenchyma by dedifferentiation, so both are redifferentiated tissues.\nCork and phelloderm are produced by cork cambium, which also arises by dedifferentiation. Both are redifferentiated.\nPrimary phloem and root-cap cells come directly from apical meristems, so they are ordinary differentiation.\nThe count is 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (plant/practice) with List-II (effect of gibberellin applied):\nList-I\n(A) Grapes\n(B) Apple\n(C) Juvenile conifers\n(D) Beet and cabbage\nList-II\n(i) Hastened maturity leading to early seed production\n(ii) Bolting\n(iii) Increased length of stalks\n(iv) Fruit elongation and improved shape\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A–(iii): GA lengthens the axis, which is used to increase the length of grape stalks.\nB–(iv): GA makes apple fruits elongate and improves their shape.\nC–(i): spraying juvenile conifers with GA hastens maturity, so they produce seeds early.\nD–(ii): GA promotes bolting in rosette plants such as beet and cabbage.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A horticulturist has six tasks:\n(i) root rose stem cuttings\n(ii) quickly ripen stored bananas\n(iii) obtain seedless tomatoes\n(iv) delay senescence of leaves\n(v) make a cabbage bolt\n(vi) prevent premature drop of young fruits\nFor how many of these tasks would an auxin be the appropriate plant growth regulator?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Auxin: auxins initiate rooting in stem cuttings.\n(ii) Not auxin: ripening of fruits is caused by ethylene.\n(iii) Auxin: auxins induce parthenocarpy in tomato.\n(iv) Not auxin: cytokinins delay leaf senescence through nutrient mobilisation.\n(v) Not auxin: gibberellins promote bolting.\n(vi) Auxin: auxins prevent early fruit drop.\nSo auxin fits 3 tasks.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (plant growth regulator) with List-II (chemical nature).\nList-I:\n(a) IAA\n(b) Kinetin\n(c) Abscisic acid\n(d) $GA_3$\nList-II:\n(i) Terpene\n(ii) Carotenoid derivative\n(iii) Adenine derivative\n(iv) Indole compound","image":"","options":["a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i","a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) IAA is indole-3-acetic acid, an indole compound, so a-iv.\n(b) Kinetin is $N^6$-furfurylamino purine, an adenine derivative, so b-iii.\n(c) ABA is a derivative of carotenoids, so c-ii.\n(d) Gibberellic acid ($GA_3$) is a terpene, so d-i.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"The rise in the rate of respiration that occurs during the ripening of fruits under the influence of ethylene is termed:","image":"","options":["Bolting","Respiratory climactic","Apical dominance","Photorespiration"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Ethylene is highly effective in fruit ripening and enhances the respiration rate during ripening.\nNCERT calls this rise in respiration rate the respiratory climactic.\nBolting is internode elongation caused by gibberellins. Apical dominance is the suppression of lateral buds by the apical bud.\nPhotorespiration is unrelated to ripening.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (condition for growth) with List-II (its role) and choose the correct option.\nList-I:\nA. Water\nB. Oxygen\nC. Nutrients\nD. Temperature\nList-II:\n(i) Required for synthesis of protoplasm and act as a source of energy\n(ii) Deviation from the optimum range could be detrimental to survival\n(iii) Maintains turgidity that helps extension growth\n(iv) Helps in releasing metabolic energy for growth activities","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Plant cells grow by enlargement, and the turgidity that water provides helps extension growth.\nB-(iv): Oxygen helps release the metabolic energy needed for growth.\nC-(i): Macro- and micronutrients are needed to synthesise protoplasm and also act as an energy source.\nD-(ii): Every plant has an optimum temperature range, and deviating from it may be detrimental.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Cells in the meristematic phase of growth have thin, primary, cellulosic walls with abundant plasmodesmatal connections.\nStatement II: The zone of maturation lies between the root apex and the zone of elongation.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. Meristematic cells are rich in protoplasm and have large nuclei. Their walls are thin, primary and cellulosic, with many plasmodesmata.\nStatement II is false. Moving away from the apex, the order is meristematic zone, then elongation zone, then maturation zone. The maturation zone therefore lies beyond the elongation zone, not between it and the apex.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following features are characteristic of cells in the phase of elongation?\n(i) Increased vacuolation\n(ii) Cell enlargement\n(iii) Thin primary walls with abundant plasmodesmata as the defining feature\n(iv) New cell wall deposition\n(v) Maximal wall thickening and protoplasmic modifications","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: increased vacuolation is an elongation feature.\n(ii) Yes: cell enlargement is an elongation feature.\n(iii) No: thin primary walls with abundant plasmodesmata define the meristematic phase.\n(iv) Yes: new cell wall deposition is an elongation feature.\n(v) No: maximal wall thickening and protoplasmic modifications belong to the maturation phase.\nSo three features apply.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (plant growth regulator) with List-II (chemical nature).\nList-I:\nA. Indole-3-acetic acid\nB. Kinetin\nC. Abscisic acid\nD. Gibberellic acid ($GA_3$)\nList-II:\n(i) Terpene\n(ii) Carotenoid derivative\n(iii) Indole compound\n(iv) Adenine derivative","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A: IAA is an indole compound, so A-(iii).\nB: Kinetin ($N^6$-furfurylamino purine) is an adenine derivative, so B-(iv).\nC: ABA is a carotenoid derivative, so C-(ii).\nD: $GA_3$ is a terpene, so D-(i).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["In coriander, leaves of the juvenile plant differ in shape from those of the mature plant","Heterophylly in larkspur and in buttercup are both examples of plasticity, although they arise from different causes","Genetic factors are intracellular intrinsic factors, while PGRs are intercellular intrinsic factors","Extrinsic factors such as light and temperature act entirely independently of PGRs in controlling plant development"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: coriander shows heterophylly linked to phase of life.\n(2) Correct: larkspur heterophylly is linked to phase of life, buttercup heterophylly to the environment. Both are plasticity.\n(3) Correct: this is how NCERT classifies intrinsic factors.\n(4) Incorrect: NCERT states that many extrinsic factors, such as temperature and light, control growth and development via PGRs.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match the following:\nA. IAA — i. Herring sperm DNA\nB. ABA — ii. Bolting\nC. Ethylene — iii. Stomatal closure\nD. GA — iv. Weed-free lawns\nE. Cytokinins — v. Ripening of fruits","image":"","options":["A – iv, B – iii, C – v, D – ii, E – i","A – v, B – iii, C – iv, D – ii, E – i","A – iv, B – i, C – iv, D – iii, E – ii","A – v, B – iii, C – ii, D – i, E – iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. IAA (auxin), e.g. 2,4-D, kills dicot weeds, giving weed-free lawns: A–iv.\nB. ABA causes stomatal closure: B–iii.\nC. Ethylene promotes ripening of fruits: C–v.\nD. GA promotes bolting (internode elongation before flowering) in rosette plants: D–ii.\nE. Kinetin, a cytokinin, was first isolated from autoclaved herring sperm DNA: E–i.\nOnly option (1) gives this set of matches.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 15 (MCQ Q4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the lateral meristems, vascular cambium and cork cambium, appear later in life and increase the girth of organs in:","image":"","options":["Monocotyledonous plants only","Dicotyledonous plants and gymnosperms","Bryophytes and pteridophytes","All flowering plants, including grasses"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Vascular cambium and cork cambium are lateral meristems.\nNCERT notes that they appear later in the life of dicotyledonous plants and gymnosperms.\nTheir activity increases the girth of organs. This is called secondary growth.\nTypical monocots such as grasses lack these lateral meristems, so options naming monocots or grasses are wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A root is 4 cm long at the start of observation. It elongates at a constant rate of 0.5 cm per day, showing arithmetic growth. What will its length be after 10 days?","image":"","options":["5 cm","9 cm","14 cm","20 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $L_0$ = 4 cm, r = 0.5 cm/day, t = 10 days.\nFormula: arithmetic growth gives $L_t = L_0 + rt$.\nSubstituting: $L_t = 4 + 0.5 \\times 10 = 4 + 5$.\nSo $L_t$ = 9 cm.\nThe option 5 cm is only the increase in length, not the total length.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Kinetin, the first cytokinin to be discovered, was obtained from which source?","image":"","options":["Coconut milk","Autoclaved herring sperm DNA","Corn kernels","Human urine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cytokinins were discovered as kinetin, obtained from autoclaved herring sperm DNA.\nCoconut milk and corn kernels are sources of zeatin, a natural cytokinin, not of kinetin.\nHuman urine is the source from which auxin was first isolated.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which parameter is used to express the growth of a pollen tube?","image":"","options":["Increase in surface area","Increase in cell number","Increase in length","Increase in dry weight"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A pollen tube grows as an elongating tube, so NCERT measures its growth in terms of its length.\nSurface area is the parameter used for a dorsiventral leaf, not a pollen tube.\nCell number is used for a dividing tissue such as the maize root apex.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A dry piece of wood swells when it is placed in water. According to the NCERT definition of growth, how should this be described?","image":"","options":["It is growth, because the volume of the wood increases measurably","It is not growth, because it is a reversible physical uptake of water that involves no metabolic activity","It is growth, because its fresh weight increases, and fresh weight is a valid growth parameter","It is secondary growth, because wood is produced by the vascular cambium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines growth as an irreversible, permanent increase in size.\nIt also states that growth is accompanied by metabolic processes that use energy.\nDry wood is dead tissue. It only soaks up water (imbibition), and the swelling reverses when the wood dries.\nSo the swelling is not growth.\nFresh weight is a parameter used to measure growth in living organs. A rise in fresh weight alone does not mean growth has occurred.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the term 'development' in plants refers to:","image":"","options":["Only the irreversible increase in size of an organ","All changes an organism goes through in its life cycle, from germination of the seed to senescence","Only the structural changes in the cell wall during maturation","Only the regaining of cell division by differentiated cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Development covers all changes an organism goes through during its life cycle, from seed germination to senescence.\nOption (1) describes growth alone, which is only one part of development.\nOption (3) describes part of differentiation.\nOption (4) describes dedifferentiation.\nBroadly, development is the sum of growth and differentiation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Spraying juvenile conifers with gibberellins leads to early seed production.\nStatement II: Gibberellins lengthen the juvenile period of conifers by delaying their maturity.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: GA-treated juvenile conifers produce seeds early.\nStatement II is incorrect: GAs hasten the maturity period of conifers. They shorten the juvenile phase rather than lengthen it.\nThe tempting confusion comes from GA delaying senescence in fruits. Senescence is a different process from maturity.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following effects are brought about by ethylene (or its source, ethephon)?\n(i) Horizontal growth of seedlings\n(ii) Apical hook formation in dicot seedlings\n(iii) Bolting in cabbage\n(iv) Breaking of seed and bud dormancy\n(v) Delay of leaf senescence through nutrient mobilisation\n(vi) Promotion of female flowers in cucumber\n(vii) Induction of flowering in mango\n(viii) Increase in stem length of sugarcane","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i), (ii), (iv): Ethylene effects.\n(vi): Ethephon promotes female flowers in cucumber.\n(vii): Ethylene induces flowering in mango.\n(iii) and (viii): Bolting and sugarcane stem elongation are gibberellin effects.\n(v): Delay of senescence through nutrient mobilisation is a cytokinin effect.\nFive effects are due to ethylene.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Ethephon is applied to a cucumber crop by farmers. What is the main purpose of this treatment?","image":"","options":["To promote the formation of female flowers and so increase yield","To promote the formation of male flowers so that pollination improves","To delay leaf senescence by mobilising nutrients","To induce parthenocarpy by preventing fertilisation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Ethephon is the most widely used source of ethylene. In aqueous solution it is absorbed, transported within the plant and releases ethylene slowly.\nNCERT states that ethephon promotes female flowers in cucumbers. Female flowers develop into fruits, so the yield rises.\nOption (2) reverses this effect, because ethephon favours female flowers, not male ones.\nDelaying leaf senescence is a cytokinin effect, and inducing parthenocarpy in tomato is an auxin effect.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which chemical property does NCERT state is shared by all gibberellins?","image":"","options":["They are all gaseous","They are all acidic","They are all adenine derivatives","They are all indole compounds"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states plainly that all GAs are acidic.\nChemically, gibberellins such as $GA_3$ are terpenes.\nAdenine derivatives (kinetin) are cytokinins. Indole compounds (IAA) are auxins. The gaseous PGR is ethylene.\nSo only 'acidic' is a property common to every gibberellin.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Kinetin is an adenine derivative chemically described as $N^6$-furfurylamino purine.\nStatement II: Gibberellic acid ($GA_3$) is an indole compound, like IAA.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT describes kinetin as an adenine derivative, $N^6$-furfurylamino purine.\nStatement II is incorrect: $GA_3$ is a terpene. Only IAA (and related auxins) are indole compounds.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A cell differentiating into a tracheary element undergoes which of the following changes?","image":"","options":["It gains extra protoplasm and keeps a thin primary cellulosic wall","It loses its protoplasm and develops a strong, elastic, lignocellulosic secondary wall","It loses its cell wall and keeps an active nucleus","It develops a suberised wall and stores food in its protoplasm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, a cell forming a tracheary element loses its protoplasm.\nIt also develops a very strong, elastic, lignocellulosic secondary wall.\nThese features let it carry water over long distances even under extreme tension.\nThe other options either keep the protoplasm or describe a wall type that does not suit water conduction.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Kinetin is a naturally occurring cytokinin found widely in plants.\nStatement II: Zeatin, a cytokinin, was isolated from corn kernels and coconut milk.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Kinetin was obtained from autoclaved herring sperm DNA and does not occur naturally in plants.\nStatement II is correct. The search for natural cytokinin-like substances led to zeatin, isolated from corn kernels and coconut milk.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Leaf A has an initial area of 5 $cm^2$ and leaf B has an initial area of 50 $cm^2$. In the same time interval, each leaf increases its area by 5 $cm^2$. What is the ratio of the relative growth rate of A to that of B?","image":"","options":["1","10","0.1","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: both leaves show the same absolute increase of 5 $cm^2$ in the same time. Initial areas are 5 $cm^2$ (A) and 50 $cm^2$ (B).\nFormula: relative growth rate = increase ÷ initial size (per unit time).\nSubstituting: A = 5/5 = 1 (100%); B = 5/50 = 0.1 (10%).\nSo the ratio of A to B = 1/0.1 = 10.\nThe tempting answer 1 is the ratio of absolute growth rates, not relative ones.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, ethylene is synthesised in large amounts by:","image":"","options":["Tissues undergoing senescence and ripening fruits","Root apices and developing shoot buds only","Dormant seeds under water stress","Shoot apical meristems of young seedlings"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Ethylene is a simple gaseous PGR produced in large amounts by senescing tissues and ripening fruits.\nRoot apices and developing shoot buds are sites of cytokinin synthesis, which happens wherever rapid cell division occurs.\nGrowing apices of stems and roots produce auxins.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"In beet and cabbage, gibberellins promote 'bolting'. What does bolting mean?","image":"","options":["Formation of adventitious roots on the stem","Shedding of older leaves before flowering","Internode elongation just before flowering","Swelling of the axis and apical hook formation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Bolting is internode elongation that occurs just prior to flowering.\nGibberellins promote it in beet, cabbage and many other plants with a rosette habit.\nThe compressed rosette then elongates into a tall flowering stalk.\nAxis swelling and apical hook formation are effects of ethylene. Rooting in stem cuttings is an auxin effect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are effects of abscisic acid described in NCERT?\n(i) Inhibits seed germination\n(ii) Stimulates closure of stomata\n(iii) Breaks bud dormancy\n(iv) Plays a role in seed maturation\n(v) Delays leaf senescence by nutrient mobilisation\n(vi) Increases tolerance to stresses\n(vii) Promotes bolting in rosette plants","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: ABA inhibits seed germination.\n(ii) Correct: ABA stimulates stomatal closure.\n(iii) Incorrect: ethylene breaks bud dormancy; ABA induces dormancy.\n(iv) Correct: ABA plays a role in seed development and maturation.\n(v) Incorrect: delaying senescence through nutrient mobilisation is a cytokinin effect.\n(vi) Correct: ABA increases stress tolerance.\n(vii) Incorrect: bolting is promoted by gibberellins.\nThat makes four correct statements.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A growing root tip is marked with ink lines at equal intervals using the parallel line technique. After some time, the gap between the lines has widened most in the region just behind the apex. The lines farther back show little change. Which conclusion is correct?","image":"","options":["The widened region is the meristematic zone, where cell division alone lengthens the root","The widened region is the zone of elongation, where cells enlarge with increased vacuolation","The widened region is the zone of maturation, where wall thickening increases length","Lengthening occurs uniformly along the root, so unequal spacing must be an artefact"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In the parallel line technique, equal marks spread apart only where cells increase in length.\nNCERT's figure shows that the zones immediately behind the apex have elongated most. This is the zone of elongation, where cells enlarge and become vacuolated.\nThe extreme tip divides but adds little length by itself.\nThe region farther back is maturing; its cells no longer elongate, so the marks barely move.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Tea bushes and hedges are regularly pruned at their shoot tips to make them dense and bushy.\nReason (R): In most higher plants, the growing apical bud suppresses the growth of the axillary buds. Removing it allows the lateral buds to grow into branches.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that decapitation is widely applied in tea plantations and hedge-making.\nR is true: the inhibition of lateral buds by the growing apical bud is called apical dominance. Removing the shoot tip lets the lateral buds grow.\nThe lateral buds released by pruning form many branches. This is exactly why the bushes become dense, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Differentiation\n(b) Dedifferentiation\n(c) Redifferentiation\n(d) Open differentiation\nList-II\n(i) Cork cambium producing cork cells\n(ii) Cells from one meristem forming root cap and epidermis depending on position\n(iii) Meristem-derived cell maturing into a tracheary element\n(iv) Parenchyma cell forming cork cambium","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Differentiation is maturation of meristem-derived cells, such as a tracheary element, so a-iii.\n(b) Dedifferentiation is a differentiated parenchyma cell regaining division and forming cork cambium, so b-iv.\n(c) Redifferentiation is that dedifferentiated meristem producing cells (cork) that mature again, so c-i.\n(d) Open differentiation is one meristem giving different cell types depending on position, so d-ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match the auxin effects in List-I with the related example or application in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Initiation of roots in stem cuttings\n(b) Promotion of flowering\n(c) Induction of parthenocarpy\n(d) Selective killing of weeds\nList-II\n(i) Pineapple\n(ii) Tomato\n(iii) Plant propagation\n(iv) Weed-free lawns","image":"","options":["a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv","a-iii, b-ii, c-i, d-iv","a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv","a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Rooting of stem cuttings is widely used for plant propagation, so a-iii.\n(b) Auxins promote flowering, for example in pineapple, so b-i.\n(c) Auxins induce parthenocarpy, for example in tomato, so c-ii.\n(d) 2,4-D kills dicot weeds and is used to make weed-free lawns, so d-iv.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"In tea plantations, the shoot tips of bushes are regularly plucked off. What is the main effect of this practice on the plant?","image":"","options":["The apical bud grows faster","Leaf senescence is delayed","Flowering is induced early","Lateral buds grow into branches, making the bush denser"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The growing apical bud inhibits the growth of the lateral (axillary) buds. This is called apical dominance.\nRemoving the shoot tip (decapitation) removes this inhibition, so the lateral buds grow into branches.\nThe result is a bushy plant that bears more leaves. The same principle is used in hedge-making.\nThe apical bud cannot grow faster, because it has been removed.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A root tip is marked with equally spaced parallel ink lines. After some time, the spaces between the lines just behind the apex (proximal to the dividing region) have widened the most. Which feature best describes the cells in this region of maximum widening?","image":"","options":["Dense protoplasm, large conspicuous nuclei and thin primary cellulosic walls with abundant plasmodesmata","Maximal wall thickening and protoplasmic modifications","Loss of protoplasm and deposition of lignocellulosic secondary walls","Increased vacuolation, cell enlargement and new cell wall deposition"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The parallel line technique detects zones of elongation. The spaces widen most where cells are elongating.\nThis zone lies just proximal to the meristematic zone.\nIts cells show increased vacuolation, cell enlargement and new cell wall deposition, so option (4) fits.\nOption (1) describes the meristematic phase.\nOption (2) describes the maturation phase.\nOption (3) describes a differentiated tracheary element.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Coconut water contains","image":"","options":["ABA","Auxin","Cytokinin","Gibberellin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): cytokinins were discovered as substances that promote cytokinesis. Natural cytokinins such as zeatin occur in coconut milk, which is the liquid endosperm.\nThat is why coconut water is added to plant tissue culture media to stimulate cell division. So (3) fits.\nAuxin (2) is the tempting option because it is also used in tissue culture. However, auxin is not the characteristic hormone of coconut water.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 15 (MCQ Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A cell derived from the procambium differentiates into a tracheary element. Which change accompanies this differentiation?","image":"","options":["It loses its protoplasm and develops a strong, elastic, lignocellulosic secondary wall","It keeps a dense protoplasm and a thin primary cellulosic wall with many plasmodesmata","It regains the capacity to divide and forms a lateral meristem","It loses its cell wall and keeps only a living protoplast to conduct water"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT gives the tracheary element as an example of a major structural change during differentiation.\nIts cells lose their protoplasm and lay down a very strong, elastic, lignocellulosic secondary wall, so they can carry water over long distances even under extreme tension.\nOption (2) describes meristematic cells, not mature tracheary elements.\nOption (3) describes dedifferentiation, and option (4) is the opposite of what happens.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"The naturally occurring cytokinin zeatin was isolated from:","image":"","options":["Tips of oat coleoptiles","Sterile filtrates of Gibberella fujikuroi","Corn kernels and coconut milk","Ripened oranges"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Scientists searched for natural substances with cytokinin-like activity. This search led to zeatin, isolated from corn kernels and coconut milk.\nOat coleoptile tips gave auxin (F.W. Went).\nGibberella fujikuroi filtrates gave gibberellic acid.\nRipened oranges release ethylene.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Which pair of agricultural practices both depend on the ability of gibberellins to increase the length of the axis?","image":"","options":["Speeding up malting and early seed production in conifers","Increasing grape stalk length and increasing sugarcane yield","Elongating apple fruits and inducing roots in stem cuttings","Bolting in cabbage and hastening ripening of tomatoes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says GA's ability to increase axis length is used to lengthen grape stalks.\nThe same ability lengthens sugarcane stems, which raises the yield of stored sugar. So both practices in option 2 rely on axis elongation.\nOption 1: neither malting nor early seed production in conifers is an axis-elongation effect.\nOption 3: rooting of stem cuttings is an auxin effect.\nOption 4: tomato ripening is caused by ethylene/ethephon.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Chemically, kinetin (N$^6$-furfurylamino purine) is best described as:","image":"","options":["An indole compound","A terpene","A derivative of carotenoids","A modified form of adenine, a purine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT describes kinetin as a modified form of adenine, which is a purine. Its full name, N$^6$-furfurylamino purine, reflects this.\nIndole compounds are auxins such as IAA.\nTerpenes are gibberellins such as GA$_3$.\nCarotenoid derivatives include ABA.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which of the following best defines growth in plants?","image":"","options":["A reversible increase in the size of a cell due to uptake of water","An irreversible, permanent increase in size of an organ, its parts or even an individual cell","Any change in the shape of an organ without change in its size","A temporary increase in the dry weight of a seed during imbibition"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: NCERT defines growth as an irreversible, permanent increase in size of an organ, its parts or even of an individual cell.\nGrowth is generally accompanied by anabolic and catabolic processes that use energy.\nOption (1) fails because a reversible change is not growth, even if water uptake causes it.\nOptions (3) and (4) describe a change of shape only and a temporary change, so neither is a permanent increase.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (scientist) with List-II (observation leading to the discovery of a PGR).\nList-I:\n(A) Charles and Francis Darwin\n(B) E. Kurosawa\n(C) F. Skoog and co-workers\n(D) H.H. Cousins\nList-II:\n(i) A volatile substance from ripened oranges hastened the ripening of stored unripe bananas\n(ii) Callus from tobacco internodal segments proliferated only when the medium with auxin was supplemented with coconut milk, yeast extract or DNA\n(iii) Sterile fungal filtrates produced 'bakanae' symptoms in rice seedlings\n(iv) Coleoptiles of canary grass bent towards unilateral light","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The Darwins observed canary grass coleoptiles bending towards light, which led to auxin. So A-iv.\n(B) Kurosawa (1926) produced bakanae symptoms with sterile filtrates of Gibberella fujikuroi, which led to gibberellins. So B-iii.\n(C) Skoog's callus work on tobacco led to kinetin (cytokinins). So C-ii.\n(D) Cousins (1910) found a volatile substance from oranges, later identified as ethylene. So D-i.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"The photoperiod in plants is perceived at","image":"","options":["Meristem","Flower","Floral buds","Leaves"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: the duration of light and dark (photoperiod) is perceived by the leaves.\nThe leaves then send a hormonal signal, the hypothetical florigen, to the shoot apices. This signal turns the apices into flowering apices.\nWhy not (1): the meristem is where the flowering response happens. It is not where the light is sensed.\nOptions (2) and (3) are also wrong because flowers and floral buds only form after induction has occurred.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 15 (MCQ Q12)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-180","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":180,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Gardeners spray 2,4-D to prepare weed-free lawns.\nReason (R): 2,4-D kills dicotyledonous weeds but does not affect mature monocotyledonous plants.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that 2,4-D is used by gardeners to prepare weed-free lawns.\nR is true: 2,4-D is a synthetic auxin used as a herbicide. It kills dicot weeds and spares mature monocots.\nLawn grasses are monocots, so the selective action described in R is exactly why 2,4-D can clear a lawn of weeds. R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-181","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":181,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"To increase sugar production in sugarcanes, they are sprayed with","image":"","options":["IAA","Cytokinin","Gibberellin","Ethylene"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): sugarcane stores carbohydrate as sugar in its stem.\nSpraying sugarcane with gibberellins increases the length of the stem. NCERT notes this can raise yield by as much as 20 tonnes per acre. So (3) fits.\nIAA (1) is the tempting option because it also promotes elongation. However, the commercial practice described in NCERT uses gibberellins, not auxin.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 15 (MCQ Q9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-182","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":182,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"F.W. Went isolated auxin from:","image":"","options":["Tips of coleoptiles of canary grass","Tips of coleoptiles of oat seedlings","Internodal segments of tobacco stems","Ripened orange fruits"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The Darwins worked on canary grass coleoptiles to study phototropism.\nWent isolated auxin from the tips of coleoptiles of oat seedlings.\nTobacco internodal segments relate to Skoog's callus work, and ripened oranges relate to Cousins and ethylene.\nThe tempting option (1) mixes Went's work with the Darwins' plant.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-183","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":183,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Ethephon is sprayed on cucumber crops to increase yield. This works because ethephon:","image":"","options":["Induces parthenocarpy in cucumber fruits","Promotes the formation of female flowers","Delays senescence of cucumber leaves","Promotes bolting of the cucumber vine"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that ethephon promotes female flowers in cucumbers. More female flowers mean more fruits, so the yield rises.\nParthenocarpy, for example in tomato, is induced by auxins.\nDelay of leaf senescence is a role of cytokinins.\nBolting is caused by gibberellins.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-184","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":184,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"A horticulturist needs to (i) induce roots on stem cuttings, (ii) increase stem length and yield of sugarcane, (iii) thin excess fruits on cherry trees, and (iv) keep leaves green longer by delaying senescence through nutrient mobilisation. Which sequence of PGRs is correct for tasks (i) to (iv)?","image":"","options":["Cytokinin, auxin, ethephon, gibberellin","IBA (auxin), gibberellin, ethephon, cytokinin","Gibberellin, IBA (auxin), ABA, ethephon","IBA (auxin), cytokinin, gibberellin, ethephon"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Auxins such as IBA and NAA initiate rooting in stem cuttings.\n(ii) Gibberellins lengthen sugarcane stems, raising yield by up to 20 tonnes per acre.\n(iii) Ethephon releases ethylene, which accelerates abscission and thins cherry, cotton and walnut fruits.\n(iv) Cytokinins promote nutrient mobilisation and so delay leaf senescence.\nGibberellins also delay senescence, but in fruits left on the tree, not through nutrient mobilisation in leaves.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-PLANT-GROWTH-185","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Plant Growth and Development","qno":185,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Plant Growth and Development","type":"mcq","question":"Monocarpic plants are those which","image":"","options":["Bear flowers with one ovary","Flower once and die","Bear only one flower","All of the above"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: monocarpic plants flower and fruit only once in their whole life and then die.\nExamples are annuals such as wheat and rice, biennials, and some perennials such as bamboo, which may flower only once after 50–100 years.\nWhy (1) is wrong: having one ovary per flower describes flower structure, not the life pattern of the plant.\nWhy (3) is wrong: such plants usually bear many flowers in that single flowering phase. So (4) is also wrong.\nThe opposite group is polycarpic plants, such as mango, which flower many times.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 15 (MCQ Q11)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 13 · Plant Growth and Development","difficulty":"easy"}]});
